00:01:11.880 --> 00:01:13.590
All right.

00:01:13.590 --> 00:01:16.530
Let's go over
vector optimization.

00:01:16.530 --> 00:01:18.930
Now just to put
this in context, we

00:01:18.930 --> 00:01:21.060
looked at vector inequalities.

00:01:21.060 --> 00:01:23.280
In a vector inequality,
what happens

00:01:23.280 --> 00:01:25.830
is something like
that is a vector

00:01:25.830 --> 00:01:30.475
and this inequality is now to be
interpreted in a vector sense.

00:01:30.475 --> 00:01:32.850
This could state that a matrix
is positive semi-definite,

00:01:32.850 --> 00:01:34.800
a matrix variable is
positive semi-definite.

00:01:34.800 --> 00:01:36.865
That would be an example.

00:01:36.865 --> 00:01:38.490
Now when we were
doing that, we did not

00:01:38.490 --> 00:01:41.820
change the semantics of
an optimization problem.

00:01:41.820 --> 00:01:43.680
The semantics was identical.

00:01:43.680 --> 00:01:46.980
And the semantics, I'll repeat
it one more time, it's this.

00:01:46.980 --> 00:01:49.920
Semantics of an
optimization problem

00:01:49.920 --> 00:01:53.700
is if a point does not
satisfy all the constraints,

00:01:53.700 --> 00:01:56.580
if it is not feasible, it's
completely unacceptable

00:01:56.580 --> 00:01:59.550
and there's no more discussion.

00:01:59.550 --> 00:02:02.910
Among those that do satisfy
all the constraints,

00:02:02.910 --> 00:02:05.050
the one with the
least objective,

00:02:05.050 --> 00:02:08.669
which is a scalar in a
standard optimization problem,

00:02:08.669 --> 00:02:09.810
is the best one.

00:02:09.810 --> 00:02:10.800
Everybody got that?

00:02:10.800 --> 00:02:11.940
That's the semantics.

00:02:11.940 --> 00:02:13.260
There's nothing else to it.

00:02:13.260 --> 00:02:15.900
Now we're going to be talking
about vector optimization.

00:02:15.900 --> 00:02:17.760
And what we're going
to do is we're actually

00:02:17.760 --> 00:02:21.360
going to now generalize,
not constraints,

00:02:21.360 --> 00:02:24.070
but the objective
to be a vector.

00:02:24.070 --> 00:02:26.640
Now there, we're
actually going to have

00:02:26.640 --> 00:02:30.270
to mess with the semantics,
because what does it

00:02:30.270 --> 00:02:34.020
mean to say minimize a vector?

00:02:34.020 --> 00:02:35.607
Like offhand?

00:02:35.607 --> 00:02:37.440
And it turns out it can
mean lots of things.

00:02:37.440 --> 00:02:40.020
And, not surprisingly,
the main distinction

00:02:40.020 --> 00:02:44.010
is going to be things like
minimal versus minimum.

00:02:44.010 --> 00:02:48.270
Because vector vectors
are generally not

00:02:48.270 --> 00:02:52.990
the-- most orderings you have on
them are not linear orderings.

00:02:52.990 --> 00:02:54.720
OK so this is what
we're going to do.

00:02:54.720 --> 00:02:55.990
It's going to look like this.

00:02:55.990 --> 00:02:58.770
And now this one, we
can state what it is.

00:02:58.770 --> 00:03:00.480
That's its syntax.

00:03:00.480 --> 00:03:05.970
What we have not yet given
you is the semantics.

00:03:05.970 --> 00:03:07.290
I could do things like say--

00:03:07.290 --> 00:03:08.665
I mean, here would
be an example.

00:03:08.665 --> 00:03:10.620
I could say, hey, please
find an estimator.

00:03:10.620 --> 00:03:12.037
And you go, what's
your objective?

00:03:12.037 --> 00:03:16.830
And you go, make my error
covariance matrix small.

00:03:16.830 --> 00:03:18.660
Everybody got that?

00:03:18.660 --> 00:03:21.420
That is a problem
just like that.

00:03:21.420 --> 00:03:24.370
Of course, immediately
someone would say,

00:03:24.370 --> 00:03:26.250
what do you mean by small?

00:03:26.250 --> 00:03:26.820
Right?

00:03:26.820 --> 00:03:29.370
Because then there could
be lots of ways to say

00:03:29.370 --> 00:03:31.950
a covariance matrix is small.

00:03:31.950 --> 00:03:36.030
One way that would
be clear is if there

00:03:36.030 --> 00:03:38.820
was one choice for which a
covariance matrix was actually

00:03:38.820 --> 00:03:43.050
smaller than all others,
otherwise known as a minimum.

00:03:43.050 --> 00:03:46.350
Then it'd be
completely unambiguous

00:03:46.350 --> 00:03:49.290
that that's what would have
to be the solution of this.

00:03:49.290 --> 00:03:50.310
Everybody got that?

00:03:50.310 --> 00:03:51.310
But we'll get into this.

00:03:51.310 --> 00:03:52.352
This is kind of the idea.

00:03:56.700 --> 00:03:58.740
The first thing we'll
do is, let's look

00:03:58.740 --> 00:04:01.990
at the set of objective of
achievable objective value.

00:04:01.990 --> 00:04:04.320
So what that means
is we screen out

00:04:04.320 --> 00:04:06.232
all choices of x,
which are not feasible

00:04:06.232 --> 00:04:08.190
and we only look at the
ones that are feasible.

00:04:08.190 --> 00:04:11.730
They're the ones that don't get
like an immediate rejection.

00:04:11.730 --> 00:04:13.410
So we look at the
ones that are feasible

00:04:13.410 --> 00:04:15.610
and then we look at
the set of objectives.

00:04:15.610 --> 00:04:18.360
Now, if this was
a scalar, this O

00:04:18.360 --> 00:04:22.200
would be a subset
of R. In fact, it

00:04:22.200 --> 00:04:24.840
would probably be continuous.

00:04:24.840 --> 00:04:27.900
Therefore it would be an
interval of some kind.

00:04:27.900 --> 00:04:31.740
And then it's completely
simple to say what

00:04:31.740 --> 00:04:33.360
the semantics is for a scalar.

00:04:33.360 --> 00:04:35.950
It's basically the one
farthest to the left,

00:04:35.950 --> 00:04:40.200
which is the smallest
point in that interval.

00:04:40.200 --> 00:04:41.280
So that would be that.

00:04:41.280 --> 00:04:44.100
Here, this is like a weird set.

00:04:44.100 --> 00:04:47.880
For example, let's pretend
that this is with respect

00:04:47.880 --> 00:04:50.505
to R-plus to the two.

00:04:53.130 --> 00:04:56.280
Let's say that here are the
objective-- what that means is,

00:04:56.280 --> 00:05:01.020
basically, down and
to the left is good,

00:05:01.020 --> 00:05:04.500
if you take that to
be the objective.

00:05:04.500 --> 00:05:08.610
Then it could be that
O looks like this.

00:05:08.610 --> 00:05:11.970
And here you see there's a
minimum point, in which case

00:05:11.970 --> 00:05:14.880
you just simply say,
it's optimal, period.

00:05:14.880 --> 00:05:15.990
It's just optimal.

00:05:18.756 --> 00:05:20.580
That's what it
means to be optimal.

00:05:20.580 --> 00:05:22.350
Basically, that
happens pretty rarely,

00:05:22.350 --> 00:05:25.110
but it does happen in some very
famous examples in, let's say,

00:05:25.110 --> 00:05:26.760
statistics and estimation.

00:05:26.760 --> 00:05:29.640
But generally, it's not.

00:05:29.640 --> 00:05:31.590
OK

00:05:31.590 --> 00:05:37.410
It's Pareto optimal, is the name
that you would give to a point

00:05:37.410 --> 00:05:39.900
if f0 of x-- that's
a vector, remember--

00:05:39.900 --> 00:05:42.430
is a minimal value of this.

00:05:42.430 --> 00:05:46.500
And so what that means is here
are the achievable things,

00:05:46.500 --> 00:05:47.920
here's what you can do.

00:05:47.920 --> 00:05:52.560
Remember, in this one, R
plus squared is the vector,

00:05:52.560 --> 00:05:57.280
is the cone we use
to compare vectors.

00:05:57.280 --> 00:06:00.010
What that means is if you
have a point here, everything

00:06:00.010 --> 00:06:02.350
to the left and
down, these things

00:06:02.350 --> 00:06:05.740
are better than that point.

00:06:05.740 --> 00:06:09.580
So when we talked
about minimal points,

00:06:09.580 --> 00:06:12.550
we didn't assign something's
better than something.

00:06:12.550 --> 00:06:16.300
Now we are, because
we're doing minimization

00:06:16.300 --> 00:06:18.730
and we want that quote,
"small," but that means

00:06:18.730 --> 00:06:19.880
small in this vector sense.

00:06:19.880 --> 00:06:22.630
So these are the
things that are better.

00:06:22.630 --> 00:06:28.450
So for example, if there's an
x that achieves this value,

00:06:28.450 --> 00:06:30.520
and I mean the
technical name for that

00:06:30.520 --> 00:06:35.110
is a stupid choice of x,
because basically it's silly.

00:06:35.110 --> 00:06:37.490
You're like look, if
you take this thing,

00:06:37.490 --> 00:06:40.040
there's all sorts
of points over here,

00:06:40.040 --> 00:06:44.920
which actually are unambiguously
better than that choice.

00:06:44.920 --> 00:06:47.830
And this is in the
vector sense, right.

00:06:47.830 --> 00:06:53.110
The minimal points of this
set O are these, over here.

00:06:53.110 --> 00:06:55.660
It's this boundary here.

00:06:55.660 --> 00:06:57.060
And then that's open.

00:06:57.060 --> 00:06:58.660
And then this boundary here.

00:06:58.660 --> 00:07:01.230
So for every single
one of those points,

00:07:01.230 --> 00:07:03.630
there's no point
that is strictly

00:07:03.630 --> 00:07:06.780
better than that point.

00:07:06.780 --> 00:07:08.520
These are the minimal--
they're literally

00:07:08.520 --> 00:07:13.080
the minimal points of O, which
is the achievable objectives.

00:07:13.080 --> 00:07:19.590
And it kind of all really
makes perfect sense.

00:07:19.590 --> 00:07:22.230
And then, actually,
if someone says,

00:07:22.230 --> 00:07:27.240
can you compare this and this,
then the answer would be no.

00:07:27.240 --> 00:07:31.920
Then no one could say,
without further information,

00:07:31.920 --> 00:07:34.630
you couldn't say that
that's better than that.

00:07:34.630 --> 00:07:37.960
You could just-- in fact,
a very good name for Pareto

00:07:37.960 --> 00:07:38.822
is a great name.

00:07:38.822 --> 00:07:40.030
I forget which field uses it.

00:07:40.030 --> 00:07:44.080
It's called "non-dominated."

00:07:44.080 --> 00:07:45.850
A non-dominated point.

00:07:45.850 --> 00:07:49.780
Because something like this
point, here, is dominated.

00:07:49.780 --> 00:07:50.655
It's dominated.

00:07:50.655 --> 00:07:52.030
In other words,
there are choices

00:07:52.030 --> 00:07:54.190
which are simply better.

00:07:54.190 --> 00:07:56.290
They're strictly
down and to the left.

00:07:56.290 --> 00:07:58.000
OK, so that's dominated.

00:07:58.000 --> 00:08:01.720
And these the points that are
not dominated is this boundary,

00:08:01.720 --> 00:08:03.490
it's the set of minimal points.

00:08:03.490 --> 00:08:05.890
It's also called-- these
are called Pareto optimal,

00:08:05.890 --> 00:08:07.120
is another name for it.

00:08:07.120 --> 00:08:09.940
That's from economics, actually,
I think from the 1890s.

00:08:09.940 --> 00:08:11.740
Something like that.

00:08:11.740 --> 00:08:15.460
So this is not
exactly new material.

00:08:15.460 --> 00:08:20.500
So this is the new semantics of
a vector optimization problem.

00:08:20.500 --> 00:08:22.030
OK.

00:08:22.030 --> 00:08:24.790
It's clearest in the case--

00:08:24.790 --> 00:08:27.550
in what's called
multi-criterion optimization.

00:08:27.550 --> 00:08:31.120
And this is a vector problem,
where we just have the cone.

00:08:31.120 --> 00:08:34.120
And our objectives actually
have that we would literally

00:08:34.120 --> 00:08:37.480
refer to these as our
objectives, F1 up to Fq.

00:08:37.480 --> 00:08:38.650
Those are our objectives.

00:08:38.650 --> 00:08:40.600
Altogether, they become
a vector objective,

00:08:40.600 --> 00:08:45.560
but literally you'd call
these the objectives.

00:08:45.560 --> 00:08:48.030
OK, let's see what it means.

00:08:48.030 --> 00:08:51.561
So here's what it would mean.

00:08:51.561 --> 00:08:53.540
If you have these q
different objectives,

00:08:53.540 --> 00:08:56.240
and roughly speaking, you
want all of them small,

00:08:56.240 --> 00:08:58.370
that's the idea.

00:08:58.370 --> 00:09:03.860
And so if you have a point
that's optimal, what it means

00:09:03.860 --> 00:09:07.250
is it's both feasible and
it says basically what

00:09:07.250 --> 00:09:14.060
that says that there is a choice
of x where it minimizes F1

00:09:14.060 --> 00:09:16.820
and it minimizes F2
and it minimizes F3

00:09:16.820 --> 00:09:19.100
and it minimizes all of them.

00:09:19.100 --> 00:09:22.310
It simultaneously
minimizes all of them.

00:09:22.310 --> 00:09:23.240
This is an example.

00:09:23.240 --> 00:09:24.410
Like this, right.

00:09:24.410 --> 00:09:26.960
And this says that there was
no competition among them.

00:09:26.960 --> 00:09:30.830
I just-- I found a single point
that just minimized everything.

00:09:30.830 --> 00:09:34.100
I should add, this should
be kind of obvious,

00:09:34.100 --> 00:09:40.010
but I'm not aware of any problem
in a practical setting that's

00:09:40.010 --> 00:09:42.140
not a multi-criterion problem.

00:09:42.140 --> 00:09:45.780
Because a lot doesn't matter.

00:09:45.780 --> 00:09:47.960
You'll see very shortly
how to solve them.

00:09:47.960 --> 00:09:49.037
It's not a big deal.

00:09:49.037 --> 00:09:50.870
Some people actually
make this into a field,

00:09:50.870 --> 00:09:53.100
which is I find
ridiculous, because if you

00:09:53.100 --> 00:09:56.170
know how to do these things
it's extremely straightforward.

00:09:56.170 --> 00:09:58.050
So any field there
is, there are going

00:09:58.050 --> 00:10:02.040
to be trade offs among
competing objectives.

00:10:02.040 --> 00:10:03.990
If you're fitting a model
in machine learning,

00:10:03.990 --> 00:10:06.660
you'd have a loss function
and you'd have a regularizer

00:10:06.660 --> 00:10:09.420
and you'd want to
trade off among them.

00:10:09.420 --> 00:10:12.570
In control, you want
to hit your target

00:10:12.570 --> 00:10:18.270
and you could trade off fuel
and the comfort of the ride.

00:10:18.270 --> 00:10:21.930
Or something like that.

00:10:21.930 --> 00:10:25.080
Pretty much any problem I can
possibly imagine, there'll be--

00:10:25.080 --> 00:10:28.680
but then they're very easily,
as we'll see shortly, handled

00:10:28.680 --> 00:10:31.140
by straight-up optimization.

00:10:31.140 --> 00:10:34.920
Anyway, OK, back to this.

00:10:34.920 --> 00:10:37.830
By the way, this
happens very rarely.

00:10:37.830 --> 00:10:41.700
If you would say, oh, I'm
doing this circuit design.

00:10:41.700 --> 00:10:43.860
I care about the area,
I care about the power,

00:10:43.860 --> 00:10:48.045
I care about the
dynamic power, and it

00:10:48.045 --> 00:10:49.170
doesn't matter-- that's it.

00:10:49.170 --> 00:10:53.760
And I care about the maximum
power output of my amplifier.

00:10:53.760 --> 00:10:54.480
There you go.

00:10:54.480 --> 00:10:56.160
OK, so I have four objectives.

00:10:56.160 --> 00:10:58.625
And then you come back and
you go, cool, here's a design.

00:10:58.625 --> 00:11:00.000
And you go, well
what does it do?

00:11:00.000 --> 00:11:02.388
You say, oh it minimizes all
four of them simultaneously.

00:11:02.388 --> 00:11:03.180
Everybody got that?

00:11:03.180 --> 00:11:04.830
I mean, that would be awesome.

00:11:04.830 --> 00:11:07.830
But this happens
like basically never.

00:11:07.830 --> 00:11:10.240
Almost never.

00:11:10.240 --> 00:11:13.360
Pareto optimal is
actually interesting.

00:11:13.360 --> 00:11:17.420
To be Pareto optimal, what
it means is the following.

00:11:17.420 --> 00:11:19.510
It means you're non-dominated.

00:11:19.510 --> 00:11:21.490
It means you say,
well, I have an x

00:11:21.490 --> 00:11:26.260
and you get these q different
scores or objective values.

00:11:26.260 --> 00:11:29.540
Then what you know if
you are Pareto optimal is

00:11:29.540 --> 00:11:32.380
that nobody can embarrass you.

00:11:32.380 --> 00:11:34.210
Embarrass you
means, oh yeah, well

00:11:34.210 --> 00:11:36.880
I've got an x that
matches you on all

00:11:36.880 --> 00:11:38.290
and beats you on at least one.

00:11:38.290 --> 00:11:41.020
Everybody, that was
informal, but it was actually

00:11:41.020 --> 00:11:44.290
a precise statement of what
it meant to be Pareto optimal.

00:11:44.290 --> 00:11:47.290
Everybody got that?

00:11:47.290 --> 00:11:50.510
So you'd say, well, I know
how to do my production.

00:11:50.510 --> 00:11:53.770
So I will use I'll take
your plan for production

00:11:53.770 --> 00:11:55.360
and I'll say, yeah
I got a plan--

00:11:55.360 --> 00:11:59.290
mine of the 12 resources
that we both use, I match you

00:11:59.290 --> 00:12:01.240
on all and I beat you on two.

00:12:01.240 --> 00:12:05.200
That means my plan
dominates yours, which means

00:12:05.200 --> 00:12:07.360
yours is not Pareto optimal.

00:12:07.360 --> 00:12:09.190
But of course, mine
need not be, either.

00:12:09.190 --> 00:12:11.190
But that's what it means.

00:12:11.190 --> 00:12:13.710
So I think these are
ideas you've seen before.

00:12:13.710 --> 00:12:16.520
They're very simple
and, I think, intuitive

00:12:16.520 --> 00:12:20.270
but I think it's not a bad thing
to actually have it called out

00:12:20.270 --> 00:12:23.360
with a name associated with it.

00:12:23.360 --> 00:12:25.250
So the one thing
that people in a lot

00:12:25.250 --> 00:12:27.560
of areas, when you talk
about Pareto optimal,

00:12:27.560 --> 00:12:30.650
they talk about--

00:12:30.650 --> 00:12:32.840
you talk about a
trade-off, which

00:12:32.840 --> 00:12:34.850
is, I think, an extremely
good way to say it.

00:12:34.850 --> 00:12:38.360
So if you look back
over here, remember

00:12:38.360 --> 00:12:42.380
this is an optimization
problem where we're basically

00:12:42.380 --> 00:12:44.480
optimizing two things.

00:12:44.480 --> 00:12:49.010
We want we want the first
component small, so left

00:12:49.010 --> 00:12:49.790
is good.

00:12:49.790 --> 00:12:52.250
And we want the second component
small, which means down

00:12:52.250 --> 00:12:53.420
is good.

00:12:53.420 --> 00:12:57.980
And so what we would do is
if someone pointed to so--

00:12:57.980 --> 00:12:59.750
that's a stupid choice.

00:12:59.750 --> 00:13:01.550
That's an impossible choice.

00:13:01.550 --> 00:13:04.220
You know, a perfectly
nice one, but impossible.

00:13:04.220 --> 00:13:07.220
This is possible, but stupid.

00:13:07.220 --> 00:13:10.140
These are the not stupid ones
or the Pareto ones or something

00:13:10.140 --> 00:13:10.640
like that.

00:13:10.640 --> 00:13:15.870
And people would refer to this
as an optimal trade-off curve.

00:13:15.870 --> 00:13:18.530
And then if you were to
choose these two points

00:13:18.530 --> 00:13:22.160
and say let's discuss,
let's compare,

00:13:22.160 --> 00:13:25.068
and you would say, well neither
is better than the other,

00:13:25.068 --> 00:13:26.360
because they're not comparable.

00:13:26.360 --> 00:13:28.818
It is not the case
that one-- and then

00:13:28.818 --> 00:13:30.110
you'd say, well, what happened?

00:13:30.110 --> 00:13:33.530
Then you'd say, well, if I was
going to compare this point

00:13:33.530 --> 00:13:38.030
and this point, what I would
say is this point up here beats

00:13:38.030 --> 00:13:42.370
this one in the first
objective but pays

00:13:42.370 --> 00:13:45.670
for it with the second,
because it's higher.

00:13:45.670 --> 00:13:48.370
OK and so that's and
that actually gets you

00:13:48.370 --> 00:13:50.680
kind of to some
economics, I mean

00:13:50.680 --> 00:13:52.870
a simple economic
interpretation,

00:13:52.870 --> 00:13:57.820
where you are exchanging
between the two objectives, sort

00:13:57.820 --> 00:13:59.710
of paying for one
with the other.

00:13:59.710 --> 00:14:01.810
Everybody got-- these
are all just vague ideas,

00:14:01.810 --> 00:14:05.030
but they're about to be
not vague, very soon.

00:14:05.030 --> 00:14:05.530
OK.

00:14:10.620 --> 00:14:11.820
Here's an example.

00:14:11.820 --> 00:14:15.840
It's a bi-criterion problem
with two objectives.

00:14:15.840 --> 00:14:17.160
And in fact, the objective--

00:14:17.160 --> 00:14:19.630
I mean, this is basically
a least squares objective.

00:14:19.630 --> 00:14:23.940
So this could be
your least squares

00:14:23.940 --> 00:14:26.160
training error or something.

00:14:26.160 --> 00:14:28.230
And then this would
be like if you're

00:14:28.230 --> 00:14:32.123
doing ridge regression,
which we'll get to later.

00:14:32.123 --> 00:14:33.540
But actually, this
is interesting.

00:14:33.540 --> 00:14:36.060
What this does is it says--

00:14:36.060 --> 00:14:38.730
the semantics of this is
someone saying, choose an x.

00:14:38.730 --> 00:14:40.230
And you say, how
will you be judged?

00:14:40.230 --> 00:14:42.630
And you say you will be
judged by two numbers--

00:14:42.630 --> 00:14:46.020
number one, this thing you
want to call that your tracking

00:14:46.020 --> 00:14:48.870
error or training error
whatever you like, I don't care.

00:14:48.870 --> 00:14:50.580
You'll be judged
by that one number.

00:14:50.580 --> 00:14:53.970
And you'd say, I want you
to please try to satisfy,

00:14:53.970 --> 00:14:56.250
approximately, Ax equals b.

00:14:56.250 --> 00:14:58.530
Oh, but at the same time, I
don't want x to be too big

00:14:58.530 --> 00:15:01.890
and that's the second
number you are judged by.

00:15:01.890 --> 00:15:07.230
And so this is an
example of that set O.

00:15:07.230 --> 00:15:10.687
And so what it says is we,
well we didn't do it this way,

00:15:10.687 --> 00:15:12.520
because you can actually
calculate that set.

00:15:12.520 --> 00:15:13.460
But it doesn't matter.

00:15:13.460 --> 00:15:17.800
But in principle, what we
did is we took all x in Rn.

00:15:17.800 --> 00:15:19.780
We evaluated that
number and that number.

00:15:19.780 --> 00:15:20.740
That's a two-vector.

00:15:20.740 --> 00:15:23.140
And I put a dark spot
wherever that happened.

00:15:23.140 --> 00:15:23.930
Everybody got it?

00:15:23.930 --> 00:15:25.305
And so this is
what's achievable.

00:15:25.305 --> 00:15:29.230
Now everything up here
is ridiculous and silly,

00:15:29.230 --> 00:15:33.730
because the semantics is
left is good, down is good.

00:15:33.730 --> 00:15:36.190
So it turns out the only
ones you should possibly

00:15:36.190 --> 00:15:40.840
ever consider are either
the minimal ones in O

00:15:40.840 --> 00:15:43.150
or the Pareto optimal ones
or something like that

00:15:43.150 --> 00:15:46.240
and that's this little
curve right here.

00:15:46.240 --> 00:15:48.640
Including the end points.

00:15:48.640 --> 00:15:51.520
Everybody got that?

00:15:51.520 --> 00:15:54.310
If, in fact, you cared
only in your choice

00:15:54.310 --> 00:15:56.590
of x about these
two numbers, then it

00:15:56.590 --> 00:15:58.780
would be extremely
silly to choose

00:15:58.780 --> 00:16:02.268
any choice of x other than
the ones on that line.

00:16:02.268 --> 00:16:04.060
And then the way we
would say this is, we'd

00:16:04.060 --> 00:16:06.430
say things like if
you wanted to compare

00:16:06.430 --> 00:16:09.070
this point and this
point, we would say,

00:16:09.070 --> 00:16:12.430
oh you just traded
off fitting error

00:16:12.430 --> 00:16:15.383
with the size of your parameters
or however you want to say it.

00:16:15.383 --> 00:16:16.550
That's how you would say it.

00:16:16.550 --> 00:16:19.900
OK, so again, this is
all kind of obvious,

00:16:19.900 --> 00:16:23.470
but this is what it
looks like, right.

00:16:23.470 --> 00:16:23.970
OK.

00:16:26.710 --> 00:16:28.570
Here's a famous one.

00:16:28.570 --> 00:16:33.190
So you're doing
portfolio construction.

00:16:33.190 --> 00:16:34.990
So I'll say a
little bit about it

00:16:34.990 --> 00:16:40.000
just so everybody is
kind of on the same page.

00:16:40.000 --> 00:16:43.540
So x-- that's an
investment portfolio,

00:16:43.540 --> 00:16:46.660
and it's usually
normalized to one.

00:16:46.660 --> 00:16:49.180
Actually, this is the case
where they're all non-negative

00:16:49.180 --> 00:16:50.138
and they add up to one.

00:16:50.138 --> 00:16:52.030
So this is a set
of mixture weights.

00:16:52.030 --> 00:16:54.700
And basically, it tells you
what fraction of your wealth

00:16:54.700 --> 00:16:57.010
you invest in each
of these things.

00:16:57.010 --> 00:16:58.870
And this is used like
absolutely everywhere.

00:16:58.870 --> 00:17:02.290
It's used on a kind
of a macroscopic scale

00:17:02.290 --> 00:17:04.569
like Stanford
endowment sits there.

00:17:04.569 --> 00:17:07.210
But there, the
individual investments

00:17:07.210 --> 00:17:11.920
are like asset classes, like
US equities, European equities,

00:17:11.920 --> 00:17:13.089
alternative investments.

00:17:13.089 --> 00:17:17.619
Venture capital is
actually one of the assets

00:17:17.619 --> 00:17:19.750
in Stanford's endowment, right.

00:17:19.750 --> 00:17:20.589
So that's this.

00:17:20.589 --> 00:17:23.450
And then this is asking how
to make a mixture of it.

00:17:23.450 --> 00:17:25.569
By the way, later
we'll talk about it,

00:17:25.569 --> 00:17:29.710
but you can violate this,
and it has a meaning.

00:17:29.710 --> 00:17:31.390
So you can actually have--

00:17:31.390 --> 00:17:36.160
Oh and you can interpret
the x's as percentages.

00:17:36.160 --> 00:17:37.600
If I had four asset classes--

00:17:37.600 --> 00:17:41.780
I mean, this would be really
dumb-- but like stocks, bonds,

00:17:41.780 --> 00:17:43.570
and cash.

00:17:43.570 --> 00:17:47.140
And I could give you 60, 40,
and 0 or something, right?

00:17:47.140 --> 00:17:48.340
Or something like that.

00:17:48.340 --> 00:17:51.540
You've heard of
these things, right.

00:17:51.540 --> 00:17:54.120
But in fact, you can
actually-- in stuff

00:17:54.120 --> 00:17:57.030
that we'll see later-- you can
actually let x get negative.

00:17:57.030 --> 00:17:58.920
And you might ask, that's weird.

00:17:58.920 --> 00:18:02.940
What does it mean to hold
-5% of your portfolio

00:18:02.940 --> 00:18:07.800
in, I don't know,
it doesn't matter,

00:18:07.800 --> 00:18:10.260
medium-term corporate bonds?

00:18:10.260 --> 00:18:13.020
Everybody see what I'm saying?

00:18:13.020 --> 00:18:14.400
I can explain again.

00:18:14.400 --> 00:18:16.710
By the way, all of
this, for most of you,

00:18:16.710 --> 00:18:19.290
this is nothing but
cultural enrichment.

00:18:19.290 --> 00:18:23.640
If you ever bump into someone
who is interested in this,

00:18:23.640 --> 00:18:25.390
you know what they're
talking about.

00:18:25.390 --> 00:18:27.630
So if you do genetics, too bad.

00:18:27.630 --> 00:18:28.488
Sorry about that.

00:18:28.488 --> 00:18:30.030
But it's not bad to
know these things

00:18:30.030 --> 00:18:33.330
right to know how optimization
is used in other fields.

00:18:33.330 --> 00:18:37.470
So what does it mean to
own -5% in, whatever,

00:18:37.470 --> 00:18:39.360
medium-term corporate bonds?

00:18:39.360 --> 00:18:45.870
What it means is you borrow
that asset from someone.

00:18:45.870 --> 00:18:48.870
You now have the obligation
to pay them back.

00:18:48.870 --> 00:18:52.840
And so you borrow it
and immediately sell it.

00:18:52.840 --> 00:18:55.270
Then later, when they come
ask, when the investment

00:18:55.270 --> 00:18:58.810
period is over, they go,
hey, give me my bond back.

00:18:58.810 --> 00:19:01.520
You buy it on the open market.

00:19:01.520 --> 00:19:04.570
If the price went down, it's
cheaper and you did well.

00:19:04.570 --> 00:19:05.548
Everybody got that?

00:19:05.548 --> 00:19:06.590
Again, you don't need it.

00:19:06.590 --> 00:19:09.560
But what's really cool about it
is all the math goes through.

00:19:09.560 --> 00:19:13.630
And so you could actually
own negative you know.

00:19:13.630 --> 00:19:16.510
Anyway, so that's the idea.

00:19:16.510 --> 00:19:17.223
Fine.

00:19:17.223 --> 00:19:18.640
This is just a
discussion of this,

00:19:18.640 --> 00:19:22.270
because this is the first
time we're seeing this.

00:19:22.270 --> 00:19:24.550
This is sort of a
very classic analysis.

00:19:24.550 --> 00:19:27.820
It's kind of like, although it's
got parts of it that were not

00:19:27.820 --> 00:19:31.810
in the original thing this
goes back to 1953 about

00:19:31.810 --> 00:19:33.790
with someone named
Harry Markowitz.

00:19:33.790 --> 00:19:34.900
So here's what it is.

00:19:34.900 --> 00:19:36.170
We care about two things.

00:19:36.170 --> 00:19:38.620
So I'm going to make
this investment portfolio

00:19:38.620 --> 00:19:44.410
and there's going
to be a return here.

00:19:44.410 --> 00:19:47.350
There's going to be a return
and the return on your--

00:19:47.350 --> 00:19:52.450
so the return might
look like this.

00:19:52.450 --> 00:19:58.450
It might be plus
0.02 -0.01, you know,

00:19:58.450 --> 00:20:00.610
and plus 0.02, or something.

00:20:00.610 --> 00:20:04.030
And this means asset
one went up 2%.

00:20:04.030 --> 00:20:05.560
So did asset three.

00:20:05.560 --> 00:20:08.660
Asset one went down 1%,
right, that's what it means.

00:20:08.660 --> 00:20:11.260
So and if you form
r transpose x,

00:20:11.260 --> 00:20:14.500
that's going to tell you
the portfolio return.

00:20:14.500 --> 00:20:19.060
So if you had 80%
here, 10% and 10%,

00:20:19.060 --> 00:20:21.430
you just work it out and
figure out how well you did.

00:20:21.430 --> 00:20:23.790
And you probably went up about--

00:20:23.790 --> 00:20:25.450
you got dinged a
little bit here--

00:20:25.450 --> 00:20:28.630
you probably went up somewhere
near 2%, but not quite.

00:20:28.630 --> 00:20:31.390
Everybody got that?

00:20:31.390 --> 00:20:36.430
The model of r is that
r is a random vector

00:20:36.430 --> 00:20:38.980
and we'll give its
first two moments.

00:20:38.980 --> 00:20:44.290
And those first two moments are
going to be the expected value

00:20:44.290 --> 00:20:45.790
and p-bar transpose.

00:20:45.790 --> 00:20:49.820
That's going to be the relative
price changes, the returns.

00:20:49.820 --> 00:20:52.570
That's this thing.

00:20:52.570 --> 00:20:54.460
I guess I'm using
bad notation here.

00:20:54.460 --> 00:20:57.610
That's fine or inconsistent.

00:20:57.610 --> 00:21:01.970
That's going to be the expected
return of the portfolio.

00:21:01.970 --> 00:21:04.750
And then the variance is going
to be x-transpose sigma x.

00:21:04.750 --> 00:21:10.630
And so you should have
some estimates of the mean

00:21:10.630 --> 00:21:12.790
and the covariance
of these things.

00:21:12.790 --> 00:21:14.950
Everybody following this?

00:21:14.950 --> 00:21:17.800
You care about two
things, which is

00:21:17.800 --> 00:21:21.940
would like a portfolio, which
on average kind of goes up,

00:21:21.940 --> 00:21:24.328
so you want high mean return.

00:21:24.328 --> 00:21:26.620
A lot of people drop the mean
and just say high return.

00:21:30.440 --> 00:21:33.250
Of course, that doesn't
tell you that every day it's

00:21:33.250 --> 00:21:33.950
going to go up.

00:21:33.950 --> 00:21:37.660
It just tells you that, if you
believe the statistical models,

00:21:37.660 --> 00:21:39.580
which by the way,
are not very good,

00:21:39.580 --> 00:21:43.660
and if you were to wind back
and repeat that day 10,000 times

00:21:43.660 --> 00:21:46.010
and take the average,
it would have gone up.

00:21:46.010 --> 00:21:48.430
But since the
latter is impossible

00:21:48.430 --> 00:21:53.200
and the first is also false,
that that's the rough idea.

00:21:53.200 --> 00:21:55.550
And this thing,
that's the variance.

00:21:55.550 --> 00:21:57.550
That measures-- actually,
that's called the risk

00:21:57.550 --> 00:21:58.788
of the portfolio, right.

00:21:58.788 --> 00:22:01.330
And if you take the square root,
it's the standard deviation.

00:22:01.330 --> 00:22:03.280
And it says, if I'm
a portfolio manager

00:22:03.280 --> 00:22:06.010
and I wake up in the
morning, have my coffee,

00:22:06.010 --> 00:22:09.220
then try to find out
did Stanford's endowment

00:22:09.220 --> 00:22:13.240
go up or down today, then I
take the mean square value

00:22:13.240 --> 00:22:16.807
of that that's going to
be that is going to be

00:22:16.807 --> 00:22:17.890
the risk of the portfolio.

00:22:17.890 --> 00:22:21.460
It's called the volatility
or something like that.

00:22:21.460 --> 00:22:25.570
Again, this is just
so you get the idea.

00:22:25.570 --> 00:22:30.350
So this is really a
bi-criterion problem.

00:22:30.350 --> 00:22:34.450
So they've got two objectives,
which is risk and return.

00:22:34.450 --> 00:22:38.290
Well the return, I want big,
and the risk, I want small.

00:22:38.290 --> 00:22:41.080
So I could have done this
with some sick cone that

00:22:41.080 --> 00:22:44.260
was kind of like R-plus cross
minus R-plus or something

00:22:44.260 --> 00:22:45.485
like that.

00:22:45.485 --> 00:22:47.110
The problem with that
is then your head

00:22:47.110 --> 00:22:50.942
explodes I mean it's correct
but it's hard to keep track of.

00:22:50.942 --> 00:22:52.150
So we'll just do it this way.

00:22:52.150 --> 00:22:53.942
We're going to minimize
the negative return

00:22:53.942 --> 00:22:58.410
because we really want that
to be high and the variance.

00:22:58.410 --> 00:22:59.370
So that's the idea.

00:22:59.370 --> 00:23:03.210
OK that's what it looks like.

00:23:03.210 --> 00:23:08.610
And then, what you could do here
is you can actually just look

00:23:08.610 --> 00:23:11.340
through, you can walk through.

00:23:11.340 --> 00:23:15.330
There's a Pareto
surface, right, here.

00:23:15.330 --> 00:23:18.360
And those correspond to the
non-dominated portfolios.

00:23:18.360 --> 00:23:21.708
And they actually
will trade off a curve

00:23:21.708 --> 00:23:22.750
that will look like this.

00:23:22.750 --> 00:23:25.167
This is a very standard curve
it's a risk-return trade-off

00:23:25.167 --> 00:23:27.270
curve.

00:23:27.270 --> 00:23:29.160
And now, I haven't drawn it.

00:23:29.160 --> 00:23:31.500
If I was going to do this,
I would flip one sign of it

00:23:31.500 --> 00:23:32.650
and all that kind of stuff.

00:23:32.650 --> 00:23:34.608
But this is the traditional
way people show it,

00:23:34.608 --> 00:23:39.420
with return this way
and risk this way.

00:23:39.420 --> 00:23:41.080
So that's the idea.

00:23:41.080 --> 00:23:48.300
So in this, without the flips
of sign here, left is good.

00:23:48.300 --> 00:23:50.430
And up is good.

00:23:50.430 --> 00:23:52.830
And so you get something
that looks like that.

00:23:52.830 --> 00:23:54.120
Yeah, there's a question.

00:23:54.120 --> 00:23:58.440
Is this considered convex,
because the negative changes

00:23:58.440 --> 00:23:59.650
things a little bit?

00:23:59.650 --> 00:24:01.320
It is absolutely.

00:24:01.320 --> 00:24:03.750
This is convex.

00:24:03.750 --> 00:24:07.530
Because here, the rule
to, in multi-criterion,

00:24:07.530 --> 00:24:09.930
if I'm minimizing,
each objective

00:24:09.930 --> 00:24:11.520
has to be convex, right.

00:24:11.520 --> 00:24:14.970
So this is convex
quadratic, the risk.

00:24:14.970 --> 00:24:21.490
And the negative return
is affine, so it's convex.

00:24:21.490 --> 00:24:22.420
Cool?

00:24:22.420 --> 00:24:22.930
OK.

00:24:22.930 --> 00:24:24.705
But yeah, good check.

00:24:24.705 --> 00:24:26.830
I just write these things
down without saying that,

00:24:26.830 --> 00:24:29.680
but that's what it is.

00:24:29.680 --> 00:24:32.170
Anyway, so this shows
you the-- and you

00:24:32.170 --> 00:24:35.020
would describe this as the
risk-return trade-off curve.

00:24:35.020 --> 00:24:38.085
And there's even some
name which I've forgotten

00:24:38.085 --> 00:24:39.460
and I forget what
this is called.

00:24:39.460 --> 00:24:40.510
This is called the--

00:24:40.510 --> 00:24:42.760
I don't even remember anymore.

00:24:42.760 --> 00:24:44.230
Does someone here know?

00:24:44.230 --> 00:24:45.497
The name?

00:24:45.497 --> 00:24:46.580
Anyway, it doesn't matter.

00:24:46.580 --> 00:24:48.280
It's the risk-return
trade-off curve.

00:24:48.280 --> 00:24:50.320
Let's just leave
it that way, OK.

00:24:50.320 --> 00:24:53.080
Or the risk-return
optimal trade-off curve.

00:24:53.080 --> 00:24:56.050
And so, this is
what it looks like.

00:24:56.050 --> 00:25:00.310
And what this shows
is actually the--

00:25:00.310 --> 00:25:03.430
so here, I have four
assets, so they add to one.

00:25:03.430 --> 00:25:05.020
This is a stack plot.

00:25:05.020 --> 00:25:09.160
So a stack plot
says, each vertical

00:25:09.160 --> 00:25:13.240
here gives you a point on the
optimal risk-return trade-off

00:25:13.240 --> 00:25:13.750
curve.

00:25:13.750 --> 00:25:16.480
And at this point, you've
got a little bit of x1,

00:25:16.480 --> 00:25:21.200
a little bit of x2, ooh a
bunch of x3, and a bunch of x4.

00:25:21.200 --> 00:25:25.940
So over here, when you insist
on 0% return, you get x4.

00:25:25.940 --> 00:25:28.100
By the way, x4 is
probably cash, which

00:25:28.100 --> 00:25:31.190
would have no risk, right.

00:25:31.190 --> 00:25:34.490
I don't know what x1 is, but
it's probably the most risky

00:25:34.490 --> 00:25:35.780
of the assets.

00:25:35.780 --> 00:25:37.843
And so, as you take,
you would say--

00:25:37.843 --> 00:25:39.260
I'll use the
language people would

00:25:39.260 --> 00:25:43.130
use-- as you take on more
risk, you get a portfolio

00:25:43.130 --> 00:25:45.920
that, actually, at some point,
you stop holding any cash.

00:25:45.920 --> 00:25:47.480
And you only hold
this, maybe these

00:25:47.480 --> 00:25:48.710
are like bonds or something.

00:25:48.710 --> 00:25:49.760
I don't know, right.

00:25:49.760 --> 00:25:51.230
Then you start taking on--

00:25:54.020 --> 00:25:56.022
then this would be
large cap stocks,

00:25:56.022 --> 00:25:57.980
and these would be like
small cap or something.

00:25:57.980 --> 00:26:00.618
I'm just making this up,
but not entirely, right.

00:26:00.618 --> 00:26:02.160
So it would look
something like this.

00:26:02.160 --> 00:26:04.760
So this all this makes sense?

00:26:04.760 --> 00:26:07.000
So this is the picture.

00:26:07.000 --> 00:26:09.950
You'll see variations
on this sort of thing

00:26:09.950 --> 00:26:12.980
all over the place.

00:26:12.980 --> 00:26:14.340
By the way, a little bit later.

00:26:14.340 --> 00:26:16.160
I will show how you
actually compute this

00:26:16.160 --> 00:26:17.870
and for that matter
you will be doing it

00:26:17.870 --> 00:26:20.300
on your homework
that's due Friday.

00:26:20.300 --> 00:26:22.325
It's actually quite
straightforward to do this.

00:26:26.330 --> 00:26:28.580
So now we're talking about
scalarization, right.

00:26:28.580 --> 00:26:33.830
So scalarization refers to
taking a vector objective

00:26:33.830 --> 00:26:37.970
and then mapping it
down to a single score.

00:26:37.970 --> 00:26:41.420
That way, that will give
you a linear ordering.

00:26:41.420 --> 00:26:43.105
Because when we scalarize--

00:26:43.105 --> 00:26:44.480
you had a production
design and I

00:26:44.480 --> 00:26:47.840
had one-- one of ours is
better, because it comes up

00:26:47.840 --> 00:26:50.810
to a certain number.

00:26:50.810 --> 00:26:53.720
Now, let's see how to do that.

00:26:53.720 --> 00:26:57.140
Well, you'll remember from
a couple of weeks ago,

00:26:57.140 --> 00:27:01.460
we talked about what happens
if you take a vector that's

00:27:01.460 --> 00:27:05.120
positive in the dual cone
right and you minimize

00:27:05.120 --> 00:27:07.580
lambda-transpose f of x.

00:27:07.580 --> 00:27:10.150
That's guaranteed to
give you a Pareto op--

00:27:10.150 --> 00:27:12.260
a minimal point, which
we now in this context

00:27:12.260 --> 00:27:15.830
referred to as a
Pareto optimal point.

00:27:15.830 --> 00:27:20.100
It says this problem, here, is
an ordinary convex optimization

00:27:20.100 --> 00:27:20.600
problem.

00:27:20.600 --> 00:27:21.800
And there's a whole
bunch of subtleties

00:27:21.800 --> 00:27:22.800
that I'm not mentioning.

00:27:22.800 --> 00:27:23.780
Here's one.

00:27:23.780 --> 00:27:27.710
If f0 of x is
k-convex, that means

00:27:27.710 --> 00:27:32.450
it's a vector function
which is k-convex.

00:27:32.450 --> 00:27:34.550
It's convex with
respect to the cone k.

00:27:34.550 --> 00:27:38.780
If I take lambda as
positive in the dual cone,

00:27:38.780 --> 00:27:41.600
then that's a scalar function.

00:27:41.600 --> 00:27:43.917
That scalar function is convex.

00:27:43.917 --> 00:27:45.000
So that's the first thing.

00:27:45.000 --> 00:27:47.630
So this is an ordinary
old convex optimization

00:27:47.630 --> 00:27:51.830
problem and the
picture is pretty cool.

00:27:51.830 --> 00:27:55.490
It basically says, if
here's O, what I do is,

00:27:55.490 --> 00:28:00.410
let me look at level curves
of lambda-transpose f0 of x.

00:28:00.410 --> 00:28:02.720
And so those are
going to be, basically

00:28:02.720 --> 00:28:04.382
hyperplanes in this O space.

00:28:04.382 --> 00:28:05.840
And then what you
want to do is you

00:28:05.840 --> 00:28:09.500
want to go as far
as you can this way.

00:28:09.500 --> 00:28:11.790
And in this case, you
would find this point.

00:28:11.790 --> 00:28:16.040
And sure enough, it is
indeed an optimal point.

00:28:16.040 --> 00:28:18.200
And also, a lot of
other ideas come up

00:28:18.200 --> 00:28:20.510
here that you would see.

00:28:20.510 --> 00:28:24.800
You could see that right here,
this is tangent to this thing,

00:28:24.800 --> 00:28:26.930
and that roughly
means and it's got

00:28:26.930 --> 00:28:29.630
a slope that's like minus
one over lambda or something.

00:28:29.630 --> 00:28:32.060
But it basically
tells you, locally,

00:28:32.060 --> 00:28:35.120
the trade-off between the two
variables-- between sorry,

00:28:35.120 --> 00:28:37.010
the two objectives.

00:28:37.010 --> 00:28:39.920
So there's going to be--
it's like an exchange rate.

00:28:39.920 --> 00:28:40.950
We'll get to that.

00:28:40.950 --> 00:28:43.490
And in fact, it is correct
to think of the lambda

00:28:43.490 --> 00:28:45.980
here as a vector of prices.

00:28:45.980 --> 00:28:49.140
We'll get to that.

00:28:49.140 --> 00:28:53.870
So in some ways, this is making
something very intuitive kind

00:28:53.870 --> 00:28:54.950
of fancy.

00:28:54.950 --> 00:28:56.150
But that's OK.

00:28:56.150 --> 00:28:58.280
This doesn't bother me.

00:28:58.280 --> 00:29:02.720
I mean a gazillion people see
multi-criterion optimization

00:29:02.720 --> 00:29:03.890
all the time.

00:29:03.890 --> 00:29:06.125
Then all they do is
they just immediately

00:29:06.125 --> 00:29:08.000
do-- without even saying
anything-- they just

00:29:08.000 --> 00:29:10.560
say, how do we solve it?

00:29:10.560 --> 00:29:13.010
And we'll just throw
in some weights.

00:29:13.010 --> 00:29:16.260
Like you're doing regularization
in machine learning.

00:29:16.260 --> 00:29:20.480
And so you throw in
loss plus lambda.

00:29:20.480 --> 00:29:23.680
Why is this dinging at me?

00:29:23.680 --> 00:29:24.700
There we go.

00:29:24.700 --> 00:29:27.850
You throw in loss plus lambda
times your regularizer.

00:29:27.850 --> 00:29:29.030
Everybody, you've seen this.

00:29:29.030 --> 00:29:30.310
And you've seen this
in lots of-- and people

00:29:30.310 --> 00:29:31.390
they don't even say
anything about it,

00:29:31.390 --> 00:29:33.700
but just because it's
obvious and it's fine.

00:29:33.700 --> 00:29:36.580
But this is what you were doing

00:29:36.580 --> 00:29:38.710
OK, now for
multi-criterion problems,

00:29:38.710 --> 00:29:45.670
we can be a lot more
specific about what

00:29:45.670 --> 00:29:48.760
this particular
scalarization does.

00:29:48.760 --> 00:29:51.700
So what it does is it's
actually kind of cool.

00:29:51.700 --> 00:29:53.980
It basically says,
here are these things

00:29:53.980 --> 00:29:55.000
that you want small.

00:29:55.000 --> 00:29:57.010
Capital F1 all the
way to capital Fq.

00:29:57.010 --> 00:29:59.290
You want them all small, right.

00:29:59.290 --> 00:30:02.530
Then, these are positive
numbers and we can actually

00:30:02.530 --> 00:30:08.410
literally interpret the
lambdas as literally the price

00:30:08.410 --> 00:30:10.480
of that objective.

00:30:10.480 --> 00:30:13.000
That's how much you
will pay if that's 12

00:30:13.000 --> 00:30:15.070
and that's like
whatever 10 then this

00:30:15.070 --> 00:30:18.640
is going to cost you $120 bucks,
so the lambdas are literally

00:30:18.640 --> 00:30:20.390
prices here.

00:30:20.390 --> 00:30:21.890
And this would be
like, for example,

00:30:21.890 --> 00:30:24.750
if we had two production
proposals-- you say, well,

00:30:24.750 --> 00:30:25.460
I know how to--

00:30:25.460 --> 00:30:28.190
or if I'm designing
a circuit and I

00:30:28.190 --> 00:30:30.230
say I want both the
area to be small,

00:30:30.230 --> 00:30:35.240
the dynamic power to be
small, these types of things,

00:30:35.240 --> 00:30:39.350
we would just we would just set
prices for those objectives.

00:30:39.350 --> 00:30:41.150
And then this is
the total price.

00:30:41.150 --> 00:30:43.730
What's interesting there is
you can change the prices

00:30:43.730 --> 00:30:46.230
and you'll get a
different choice.

00:30:46.230 --> 00:30:50.300
In fact, this is what will
allow you to actually move along

00:30:50.300 --> 00:30:52.100
the trade-off curve
if there's two.

00:30:52.100 --> 00:30:55.280
If there's more, people call
it the trade-off surface.

00:30:55.280 --> 00:30:56.180
Everybody got this?

00:30:56.180 --> 00:30:57.920
So it's all pretty
straightforward,

00:30:57.920 --> 00:31:00.570
but it's kind of
interesting to look at.

00:31:00.570 --> 00:31:03.170
So here we go.

00:31:03.170 --> 00:31:05.920
Let's do the regularized
least square--

00:31:05.920 --> 00:31:07.090
here we go.

00:31:07.090 --> 00:31:10.270
Here's-- regularized least
squares is, I want this thing,

00:31:10.270 --> 00:31:14.050
interpreted as a fitting error,
to small and then I want also

00:31:14.050 --> 00:31:16.900
the x, which in this case, we
interpret as the parameters

00:31:16.900 --> 00:31:18.550
and let's say a
regression model,

00:31:18.550 --> 00:31:20.290
I want those to be small.

00:31:20.290 --> 00:31:22.030
We'll talk much more
about that later.

00:31:22.030 --> 00:31:25.940
But how do I do that?

00:31:25.940 --> 00:31:30.910
Well, I will take something
that's a positive two-vector.

00:31:30.910 --> 00:31:32.892
By the way, if I
multiply that vector by--

00:31:32.892 --> 00:31:35.350
I can scale it by any positive
number and I'm going to get,

00:31:35.350 --> 00:31:37.720
when I solve this problem,
I'm going to get this.

00:31:37.720 --> 00:31:39.280
It's just a multiple
of this so I

00:31:39.280 --> 00:31:42.580
can without loss of generality
make the first one one.

00:31:42.580 --> 00:31:46.480
By the way people often
call, they often interpret

00:31:46.480 --> 00:31:50.500
the first objective-- they call
that their primary objective.

00:31:50.500 --> 00:31:52.550
And then, in this
case, you'd say,

00:31:52.550 --> 00:31:56.200
well our training loss
is the primary objective

00:31:56.200 --> 00:32:00.250
and your secondary objective
is that the parameter shouldn't

00:32:00.250 --> 00:32:01.790
be big.

00:32:01.790 --> 00:32:06.770
And so basically gamma
is a translation.

00:32:06.770 --> 00:32:15.440
It's an exchange rate that tells
you how much large x irritates

00:32:15.440 --> 00:32:18.770
you compared to large residual.

00:32:18.770 --> 00:32:20.090
That's what it tells you.

00:32:20.090 --> 00:32:23.060
If it's 15, it
means I don't know.

00:32:23.060 --> 00:32:24.350
It means something, right.

00:32:24.350 --> 00:32:24.930
A lot.

00:32:24.930 --> 00:32:26.888
It depends on the units
of these things, right.

00:32:26.888 --> 00:32:29.570
By the way in many cases,
it's actually very cool

00:32:29.570 --> 00:32:33.680
to think in terms o-- to put
the physical units in there.

00:32:33.680 --> 00:32:38.390
If one objective is fuel use, it
might be in kilograms of fuel.

00:32:38.390 --> 00:32:40.820
Another objective could be
the smoothness of the ride.

00:32:40.820 --> 00:32:46.010
It could literally be something
like the RMS value of the jerk.

00:32:46.010 --> 00:32:47.100
People know what that is?

00:32:47.100 --> 00:32:47.600
Sorry.

00:32:47.600 --> 00:32:52.760
OK, that was quasi-slang,
quasi-dialect.

00:32:52.760 --> 00:32:55.490
In control and
mechanical engineering,

00:32:55.490 --> 00:32:58.760
the first you have the
position, first derivative.

00:32:58.760 --> 00:33:00.470
Second derivative
is acceleration,

00:33:00.470 --> 00:33:03.070
and the third is jerk.

00:33:03.070 --> 00:33:09.760
And then after that, the
fourth derivative is snap.

00:33:09.760 --> 00:33:10.730
Good, snap.

00:33:10.730 --> 00:33:11.230
Right.

00:33:11.230 --> 00:33:13.420
So this is just universal.

00:33:13.420 --> 00:33:15.820
For example, when you get
on a fancy modern elevator,

00:33:15.820 --> 00:33:16.790
not in this country.

00:33:16.790 --> 00:33:18.623
But if you had one that
had real technology,

00:33:18.623 --> 00:33:21.280
you got a fancy one, you
wouldn't even feel moving.

00:33:21.280 --> 00:33:24.130
The reason is they actually
have a trajectory that

00:33:24.130 --> 00:33:26.590
has minimized the snap.

00:33:26.590 --> 00:33:28.360
Everybody got that?

00:33:28.360 --> 00:33:33.320
Also, I think drone trajectories
are designed to minimize snap.

00:33:33.320 --> 00:33:35.890
So if you minimize the snap,
that's the fourth derivative.

00:33:35.890 --> 00:33:37.348
Again, this is
cultural enrichment.

00:33:37.348 --> 00:33:39.220
You don't need to know
this, but it's fun.

00:33:39.220 --> 00:33:43.600
And anybody the-- they
have three more derivatives

00:33:43.600 --> 00:33:46.060
which are never used,
but there's a good joke.

00:33:46.060 --> 00:33:47.438
Crackle, pop.

00:33:47.438 --> 00:33:47.980
There you go.

00:33:47.980 --> 00:33:49.400
So the crackle and pop.

00:33:49.400 --> 00:33:49.900
That's it.

00:33:49.900 --> 00:33:52.270
So you can really impress
your mechanical engineering

00:33:52.270 --> 00:33:56.060
or control friends by
saying, if they're saying,

00:33:56.060 --> 00:33:58.660
what are you doing, I'm making
a drone fly around the Stanford

00:33:58.660 --> 00:33:59.300
campus.

00:33:59.300 --> 00:34:00.360
And you go, cool.

00:34:00.360 --> 00:34:01.360
What are you minimizing?

00:34:01.360 --> 00:34:05.740
And they'll say,
RMS value of snap.

00:34:05.740 --> 00:34:08.230
And you go, how
about crackle or pop?

00:34:08.230 --> 00:34:10.810
And you'll get a
rise out of them.

00:34:10.810 --> 00:34:11.560
I mean, I assume.

00:34:11.560 --> 00:34:12.280
OK sorry.

00:34:12.280 --> 00:34:14.139
That was a big digression.

00:34:14.139 --> 00:34:15.670
OK we're back.

00:34:15.670 --> 00:34:17.580
OK, so this is fine.

00:34:17.580 --> 00:34:19.580
Everybody, this is just
a least squares problem.

00:34:19.580 --> 00:34:21.130
And you can solve it and
everyone here has probably

00:34:21.130 --> 00:34:22.540
seen this in some context.

00:34:22.540 --> 00:34:25.840
It's ridge regression, but
we'll get to that later, OK?

00:34:25.840 --> 00:34:26.707
Yeah.

00:34:26.707 --> 00:34:28.540
On the previous slide
there was a constraint

00:34:28.540 --> 00:34:30.940
that lambda had to
be in k-star, right?

00:34:30.940 --> 00:34:31.585
That's right.

00:34:31.585 --> 00:34:33.894
Explain the intuition
behind that.

00:34:33.894 --> 00:34:34.960
Oh.

00:34:34.960 --> 00:34:36.520
Well, OK.

00:34:36.520 --> 00:34:38.770
First, there's the math, right.

00:34:38.770 --> 00:34:42.580
When I form this, this better
be a scalar convex optimization

00:34:42.580 --> 00:34:43.210
problem.

00:34:43.210 --> 00:34:47.380
And when I have when I have
an objective function, which

00:34:47.380 --> 00:34:51.429
is a vector and it's convex
with respect to a cone k,

00:34:51.429 --> 00:34:56.394
then actually if you
form lambda-transpose f0

00:34:56.394 --> 00:34:59.200
of x when lambda is
positive in the dual cone,

00:34:59.200 --> 00:35:01.540
that's guaranteed to
be a convex function.

00:35:01.540 --> 00:35:05.490
If not, if that fails,
this need not be convex.

00:35:05.490 --> 00:35:07.580
And so this is all very
nice, well and good,

00:35:07.580 --> 00:35:10.222
but we can't solve it.

00:35:10.222 --> 00:35:11.930
I don't know if that
counts as intuition.

00:35:11.930 --> 00:35:13.610
But anyway.

00:35:13.610 --> 00:35:18.140
And the dual of R plus to
the q is R plus 2 the q.

00:35:18.140 --> 00:35:20.180
So basically it says--

00:35:20.180 --> 00:35:22.700
I mean this is a way to make
in the multi-criterion case.

00:35:22.700 --> 00:35:25.340
It's making it much more
fancy than it needs to be.

00:35:25.340 --> 00:35:28.500
It would basically say take
a bunch of positive weights,

00:35:28.500 --> 00:35:31.980
minimize the positive weighted
sum of the objectives.

00:35:31.980 --> 00:35:35.700
And the statement is you
will get a Pareto optimal

00:35:35.700 --> 00:35:36.685
point, right.

00:35:36.685 --> 00:35:37.560
You want another one?

00:35:37.560 --> 00:35:39.780
Change the weights, right.

00:35:39.780 --> 00:35:42.720
And we'll talk about what
the weights mean, too.

00:35:42.720 --> 00:35:44.085
Yeah.

00:35:44.085 --> 00:35:47.700
[INAUDIBLE] almost an
operator blue point

00:35:47.700 --> 00:35:48.905
Sorry, I missed that.

00:35:48.905 --> 00:35:52.357
Is that we can find
by varying lambda?

00:35:52.357 --> 00:35:54.900
Oh we'll get to that.

00:35:54.900 --> 00:35:58.170
So it turns out, yeah,
as you vary lambda,

00:35:58.170 --> 00:36:01.920
you will get almost all
Pareto optimal points.

00:36:01.920 --> 00:36:04.260
You won't get the limits, right.

00:36:04.260 --> 00:36:06.810
In this problem, you would
not get that dark point

00:36:06.810 --> 00:36:08.102
right there.

00:36:08.102 --> 00:36:09.810
So sorry actually--
there's going to be--

00:36:09.810 --> 00:36:11.230
this is a non-convex problem.

00:36:11.230 --> 00:36:13.620
So I shouldn't be using
that as an example, right.

00:36:13.620 --> 00:36:17.010
So we'll get to that.

00:36:17.010 --> 00:36:19.320
OK.

00:36:19.320 --> 00:36:21.720
All right.

00:36:21.720 --> 00:36:23.640
I guess we looked at this.

00:36:23.640 --> 00:36:25.410
And the risk-return
trade-off, this

00:36:25.410 --> 00:36:26.910
shows you actually
how we compute

00:36:26.910 --> 00:36:28.618
that, although there's
several other ways

00:36:28.618 --> 00:36:29.580
and I'll explain that.

00:36:29.580 --> 00:36:31.050
By the way, this has a name.

00:36:31.050 --> 00:36:32.820
If you flip the
sign on this it's

00:36:32.820 --> 00:36:34.680
p-bar transpose x plus gamma.

00:36:34.680 --> 00:36:37.200
This that's actually
called the return.

00:36:37.200 --> 00:36:39.420
If I flip the sign,
p-bar transpose x

00:36:39.420 --> 00:36:43.620
is the return, minus
gamma x-transpose sigma x.

00:36:43.620 --> 00:36:45.930
So that, when you do
that, that's the risk.

00:36:45.930 --> 00:36:48.930
Gamma times-- that's called
the risk-adjusted return.

00:36:48.930 --> 00:36:51.180
That's just a completely
standard phrase.

00:36:51.180 --> 00:36:52.080
Everybody got that?

00:36:52.080 --> 00:36:57.890
And then gamma is called the
risk-aversion parameter, right,

00:36:57.890 --> 00:37:01.580
because basically
because of the sign.

00:37:01.580 --> 00:37:04.100
In that case, you want to
maximize risk-adjusted return,

00:37:04.100 --> 00:37:08.630
and it's basically something
good, which is a return minus--

00:37:08.630 --> 00:37:11.030
that means it
penalizes you to make

00:37:11.030 --> 00:37:12.620
a choice that has high risk.

00:37:12.620 --> 00:37:15.860
And gamma tells you how
much risk irritates you

00:37:15.860 --> 00:37:17.910
and it puts you on the
scale of the return.

00:37:17.910 --> 00:37:20.160
So that's why it's called
the risk-aversion parameter.

00:37:20.160 --> 00:37:22.160
But this is not a
bad thing to know.

00:37:22.160 --> 00:37:24.230
Everybody got this?

00:37:24.230 --> 00:37:25.970
By the way, there's
plenty of other ways

00:37:25.970 --> 00:37:27.050
to solve this problem.

00:37:27.050 --> 00:37:30.800
I'm going to go back to
that and just mention it,

00:37:30.800 --> 00:37:33.380
because you'll be doing
this on your homework.

00:37:33.380 --> 00:37:35.270
You don't have to--you
could scalarize.

00:37:35.270 --> 00:37:35.930
It'll work.

00:37:35.930 --> 00:37:37.070
I guarantee it'll work.

00:37:37.070 --> 00:37:41.060
You could do another thing that
would work perfectly well here.

00:37:41.060 --> 00:37:42.290
I could sweep.

00:37:42.290 --> 00:37:44.540
Let's say I'm going to sweep
across standard deviation

00:37:44.540 --> 00:37:47.453
of error like this, right
That's a square root

00:37:47.453 --> 00:37:48.620
of this or something, right.

00:37:48.620 --> 00:37:50.630
So what I do, is
I would simply--

00:37:50.630 --> 00:37:53.420
I would minimize minus
p-bar transpose x.

00:37:53.420 --> 00:37:56.242
And then I'd put a constraint
on that and sweep the constraint

00:37:56.242 --> 00:37:57.700
value and you'd
get the same thing.

00:37:57.700 --> 00:37:58.830
It basically being--

00:37:58.830 --> 00:38:00.480
I would say, suppose your--

00:38:00.480 --> 00:38:03.910
I would say your risk
should be less than 10%.

00:38:03.910 --> 00:38:06.300
And then you simply
maximize the return.

00:38:06.300 --> 00:38:07.620
And you would get this point.

00:38:07.620 --> 00:38:13.710
And then you'd have, for
risk limit in risk limits--

00:38:13.710 --> 00:38:16.830
I don't know if I'm kind
of speaking, roughly,

00:38:16.830 --> 00:38:18.240
in Python or something.

00:38:18.240 --> 00:38:19.170
Right.

00:38:19.170 --> 00:38:23.910
You'd solve the problem and then
report the return and the risk

00:38:23.910 --> 00:38:25.120
and then you'd plot it.

00:38:25.120 --> 00:38:26.160
Everybody got that?

00:38:26.160 --> 00:38:29.070
So OK.

00:38:29.070 --> 00:38:29.970
You can do that.

00:38:29.970 --> 00:38:31.230
I think-- I forget.

00:38:31.230 --> 00:38:33.960
It asks you to get a trade-off
in some energy storage system.

00:38:33.960 --> 00:38:38.360
I think that's on
the current homework

00:38:38.360 --> 00:38:41.330
or did I just remember
that correctly?

00:38:41.330 --> 00:38:43.400
I think it is, OK.

00:38:43.400 --> 00:38:46.690
OK, so all right.

00:38:46.690 --> 00:38:48.530
That finishes that.

00:38:48.530 --> 00:38:52.790
Actually, it's a perfect
segue into the next topic.

00:38:52.790 --> 00:38:55.250
So the next topic is duality.

00:38:55.250 --> 00:38:57.940
It's actually really fun.

00:38:57.940 --> 00:39:00.250
It has to do with Lagrange
multipliers and stuff.

00:39:00.250 --> 00:39:02.830
And I don't mind saying that
the first two or three times

00:39:02.830 --> 00:39:05.620
I saw Lagrange multipliers,
I had absolutely no idea what

00:39:05.620 --> 00:39:06.370
it meant.

00:39:06.370 --> 00:39:09.460
It was taught as a behavior
and it was basically

00:39:09.460 --> 00:39:10.750
the behavior was this.

00:39:10.750 --> 00:39:15.277
Like take your constraints,
add multiple-- sorry.

00:39:15.277 --> 00:39:17.110
Take your objective,
add to it some multiple

00:39:17.110 --> 00:39:18.730
of the constraints
and you're like, cool.

00:39:18.730 --> 00:39:19.420
And you go, what those?

00:39:19.420 --> 00:39:20.320
Those are Lagrange multipliers.

00:39:20.320 --> 00:39:21.610
And you go, now what do you do?

00:39:21.610 --> 00:39:23.568
Take partial derivative
set them equal to zero.

00:39:23.568 --> 00:39:25.780
You go, but what does
it mean and they're like

00:39:25.780 --> 00:39:27.705
shut up and just do it.

00:39:27.705 --> 00:39:29.830
I mean that was kind of my
experience of it, right.

00:39:29.830 --> 00:39:31.990
And the second time it
happened, there it was again.

00:39:31.990 --> 00:39:33.410
And I was like, but
what are these things?

00:39:33.410 --> 00:39:35.200
They were like,
don't worry about it.

00:39:35.200 --> 00:39:40.570
So anyway, good news is I
hope that by next Thursday

00:39:40.570 --> 00:39:43.000
you will not be--

00:39:43.000 --> 00:39:45.213
you will understand
exactly what it means

00:39:45.213 --> 00:39:46.630
or at least this
is the first time

00:39:46.630 --> 00:39:49.420
I ever understood it was in the
context of convex optimization.

00:39:49.420 --> 00:39:51.580
OK, so let's look at this.

00:39:51.580 --> 00:39:59.180
And it starts slowly and it
just very innocently goes step

00:39:59.180 --> 00:40:00.800
by step and you'll
see after a while

00:40:00.800 --> 00:40:05.060
we start saying things that
are not remotely obvious, OK.

00:40:05.060 --> 00:40:06.072
OK, here it is.

00:40:06.072 --> 00:40:07.530
So we'll start with
the Lagrangian.

00:40:07.530 --> 00:40:10.340
Oh, by the way this
strongly suggests

00:40:10.340 --> 00:40:13.220
that this is not a new
development, right,

00:40:13.220 --> 00:40:16.292
because Lagrange was doing
this around 1820 or 1810

00:40:16.292 --> 00:40:17.250
or something like that.

00:40:17.250 --> 00:40:17.930
Or maybe even earlier.

00:40:17.930 --> 00:40:18.660
I can't remember.

00:40:18.660 --> 00:40:22.580
Anyway, so now the
truth is he probably

00:40:22.580 --> 00:40:26.220
he would recognize like
baby versions of it.

00:40:26.220 --> 00:40:30.050
And so this is just an honorary
thing, where it's attributed.

00:40:30.050 --> 00:40:32.210
It's called the Lagrangian
and I don't know.

00:40:37.430 --> 00:40:39.560
It didn't have inequality
constraints then,

00:40:39.560 --> 00:40:41.540
but whatever it's just
an honorary thing, OK.

00:40:41.540 --> 00:40:42.960
So here's what it is.

00:40:42.960 --> 00:40:45.030
So you have this
standard form problem.

00:40:45.030 --> 00:40:48.390
And by the way, a lot of the
interesting applications,

00:40:48.390 --> 00:40:51.790
the problem is not
convex, OK, so just

00:40:51.790 --> 00:40:53.500
I have a standard form problem.

00:40:53.500 --> 00:40:55.690
And I'm just going to
form the Lagrangian

00:40:55.690 --> 00:40:57.320
and the Lagrangian
is going to be this.

00:40:57.320 --> 00:40:58.850
It's actually super interesting.

00:40:58.850 --> 00:40:59.650
It goes like this.

00:40:59.650 --> 00:41:01.060
It's a single function.

00:41:01.060 --> 00:41:06.970
It's going to be the
objective plus a weighted sum

00:41:06.970 --> 00:41:12.330
of the constraints, plus
this inequality constraints,

00:41:12.330 --> 00:41:15.330
plus a weighted sum of
the equality constraints.

00:41:15.330 --> 00:41:19.170
OK, that's the Lagrangian.

00:41:19.170 --> 00:41:21.270
That's the idea.

00:41:21.270 --> 00:41:24.600
And it's kind of--

00:41:24.600 --> 00:41:27.030
then people would
give ideas like this.

00:41:27.030 --> 00:41:29.700
Oh, so one way to say
it is you would say,

00:41:29.700 --> 00:41:32.880
this is a planned
economy or something.

00:41:32.880 --> 00:41:35.340
This says these are just
rules and regulations.

00:41:35.340 --> 00:41:40.230
If you violate this
or this, then somebody

00:41:40.230 --> 00:41:42.790
in law enforcement or the SEC
is going to come after you.

00:41:42.790 --> 00:41:46.440
That's a plan,
it's a requirement.

00:41:46.440 --> 00:41:49.950
Then you'd say, oh, well this
is like the market version.

00:41:49.950 --> 00:41:52.470
And by the way, all of this
will become a bit more precise

00:41:52.470 --> 00:41:52.970
later.

00:41:52.970 --> 00:41:54.930
But the market
version says, well

00:41:54.930 --> 00:41:58.920
you know what you want to
violate that inequality?

00:41:58.920 --> 00:42:00.083
OK, that's fine.

00:42:00.083 --> 00:42:01.500
And if lambda is
positive, it just

00:42:01.500 --> 00:42:03.427
says you're going to pay for it.

00:42:03.427 --> 00:42:04.260
That's what it says.

00:42:04.260 --> 00:42:06.030
That's the idea.

00:42:06.030 --> 00:42:09.010
Also, let me just draw a
picture to show you actually

00:42:09.010 --> 00:42:15.040
how appallingly naive this
looks, because it's very weird.

00:42:18.700 --> 00:42:19.960
I'm just going to draw--

00:42:19.960 --> 00:42:21.070
let's have a problem.

00:42:21.070 --> 00:42:23.920
This is zero and I'm going to--
this is going to be f1 of x.

00:42:23.920 --> 00:42:27.750
This is just f1 of x.

00:42:27.750 --> 00:42:29.500
And I'm going to have
an objective, so I'm

00:42:29.500 --> 00:42:33.100
going to plot f0 of x here.

00:42:33.100 --> 00:42:39.250
So here, if you propose to me an
x, we will evaluate f0 and f1.

00:42:39.250 --> 00:42:41.710
And the semantics of
an optimization problem

00:42:41.710 --> 00:42:46.360
is, if you're over here,
it's completely unacceptable

00:42:46.360 --> 00:42:47.950
because you violate
the constraint.

00:42:47.950 --> 00:42:52.420
Because f1 of x is positive,
so this is unacceptable.

00:42:52.420 --> 00:42:54.580
if you're on the left
side, everything is cool

00:42:54.580 --> 00:42:57.065
and you want to go
as low as possible.

00:42:57.065 --> 00:42:59.440
In fact, that's the semantics
of an optimization problem.

00:42:59.440 --> 00:43:00.640
Everybody got this?

00:43:00.640 --> 00:43:04.270
Roughly speaking,
you're saying, here's

00:43:04.270 --> 00:43:07.930
how I can also make it
an unconstrained problem

00:43:07.930 --> 00:43:12.040
by just writing it this way,
the indicator function of f1

00:43:12.040 --> 00:43:13.690
of x is less than 0.

00:43:13.690 --> 00:43:18.430
So this is 0 if this
predicate evaluates to true

00:43:18.430 --> 00:43:21.910
and this is infinity,
if this is violated.

00:43:21.910 --> 00:43:24.610
And so solving the
constraint problem

00:43:24.610 --> 00:43:27.060
is the same as
minimizing this function.

00:43:27.060 --> 00:43:28.888
Everybody see what I mean?

00:43:28.888 --> 00:43:31.180
It's kind of silly, but that's
what-- everybody got it?

00:43:31.180 --> 00:43:33.430
OK, so I'm going to draw
the indicator function.

00:43:33.430 --> 00:43:34.310
It looks like this.

00:43:34.310 --> 00:43:37.460
So someone says, what are
you doing and you say, well,

00:43:37.460 --> 00:43:40.750
I'm converting my constrained
problem to one that's

00:43:40.750 --> 00:43:44.140
unconstrained by putting
the second term, which

00:43:44.140 --> 00:43:45.550
handles the objective.

00:43:45.550 --> 00:43:46.750
And it's a weird term.

00:43:46.750 --> 00:43:49.480
It's like a weird, emotional--
it's like a bipolar,

00:43:49.480 --> 00:43:52.660
it's either 0 or plus infinity.

00:43:52.660 --> 00:43:55.000
So it's just like,
OK that's fine.

00:43:55.000 --> 00:43:58.510
So basically that's
this picture here.

00:43:58.510 --> 00:44:01.240
OK, now let's look
at the Lagrangian.

00:44:01.240 --> 00:44:09.430
Well the Lagrangian is going to
be f0 of x plus lambda f1 of x.

00:44:09.430 --> 00:44:10.600
That's the Lagrangian.

00:44:10.600 --> 00:44:12.190
I'm going to draw that.

00:44:12.190 --> 00:44:14.580
OK, here, it looks like this.

00:44:14.580 --> 00:44:18.250
That's lambda.

00:44:18.250 --> 00:44:20.960
That slope is lambda.

00:44:20.960 --> 00:44:22.180
And now you can see--

00:44:22.180 --> 00:44:25.210
and by the way, if
the story starts,

00:44:25.210 --> 00:44:28.750
I want to develop methods
that handle constraints,

00:44:28.750 --> 00:44:32.713
but using unconstrained
optimization.

00:44:32.713 --> 00:44:34.630
And the first thing
someone does is say, yeah,

00:44:34.630 --> 00:44:37.600
problem you see this thing
I'm going to approximate it by

00:44:37.600 --> 00:44:43.460
that, you would say that is
not a very good approximation.

00:44:43.460 --> 00:44:45.390
Like everybody agrees,
it's ridiculous.

00:44:45.390 --> 00:44:47.990
So I'm just saying that the
whole beginning to this story

00:44:47.990 --> 00:44:48.770
is appalling.

00:44:48.770 --> 00:44:51.750
It just doesn't make any sense.

00:44:51.750 --> 00:44:56.490
That curve, that line is
not a good approximation

00:44:56.490 --> 00:44:57.240
of the true one.

00:44:57.240 --> 00:45:01.080
Also, it shows you all
sorts of weird differences.

00:45:01.080 --> 00:45:02.850
In the true
optimization problem,

00:45:02.850 --> 00:45:06.600
if you have a
warehouse usage limit,

00:45:06.600 --> 00:45:08.670
this is how much your f1
of x is, how much you're

00:45:08.670 --> 00:45:09.420
above your limit.

00:45:09.420 --> 00:45:11.100
And we're using warehouse space.

00:45:11.100 --> 00:45:13.500
Then basically it says
you go above the limit.

00:45:13.500 --> 00:45:14.880
Totally unacceptable.

00:45:14.880 --> 00:45:17.460
If you're below, I don't care.

00:45:17.460 --> 00:45:19.440
You could be using
all of your warehouse

00:45:19.440 --> 00:45:21.512
or I don't know you
can come in over here,

00:45:21.512 --> 00:45:23.970
which is like you got a bunch
of warehouses you're not even

00:45:23.970 --> 00:45:24.450
using.

00:45:24.450 --> 00:45:25.450
You see what I'm saying?

00:45:25.450 --> 00:45:26.100
And guess what?

00:45:26.100 --> 00:45:29.760
In the optimization
problem, there's

00:45:29.760 --> 00:45:31.710
nothing different about them.

00:45:31.710 --> 00:45:34.080
This one has a super
interesting interpretation.

00:45:34.080 --> 00:45:38.280
It's a price, because presumably
f0 is in like US dollars

00:45:38.280 --> 00:45:39.540
or something like that.

00:45:39.540 --> 00:45:42.870
And what this says is, yeah, you
want to go over your maximum?

00:45:42.870 --> 00:45:43.590
No problem.

00:45:43.590 --> 00:45:44.840
You want to operate over here?

00:45:44.840 --> 00:45:45.390
Go ahead.

00:45:45.390 --> 00:45:48.690
This says you're going to pay
for extra warehouse space.

00:45:48.690 --> 00:45:49.950
Everybody got that?

00:45:49.950 --> 00:45:53.380
But then, there's also a flip
side, which is over here.

00:45:53.380 --> 00:45:57.310
You will be subsidized
for having extra.

00:45:57.310 --> 00:46:00.580
You can rent out warehouse
space you're not using

00:46:00.580 --> 00:46:02.710
and actually generate income.

00:46:02.710 --> 00:46:04.420
So it's really quite different.

00:46:04.420 --> 00:46:06.640
Everybody see this?

00:46:06.640 --> 00:46:09.550
This is just to give you
the idea of how all this is

00:46:09.550 --> 00:46:10.570
going to go together.

00:46:10.570 --> 00:46:11.890
But these are the ideas.

00:46:11.890 --> 00:46:13.360
They're very basic.

00:46:13.360 --> 00:46:15.010
Everybody got this?

00:46:15.010 --> 00:46:16.270
OK, so that's the idea.

00:46:16.270 --> 00:46:19.990
And people would blabber about a
market economy versus a planned

00:46:19.990 --> 00:46:20.570
economy.

00:46:20.570 --> 00:46:22.150
And so on and so forth.

00:46:22.150 --> 00:46:24.230
But this is kind of the idea.

00:46:24.230 --> 00:46:28.090
Also, that strongly
hints these weights,

00:46:28.090 --> 00:46:30.553
these Lagrange
multipliers they're

00:46:30.553 --> 00:46:32.470
going to be-- they're
going to, in many cases,

00:46:32.470 --> 00:46:34.570
turn out to be prices.

00:46:34.570 --> 00:46:36.580
OK, so that's what
they're going to be.

00:46:36.580 --> 00:46:43.700
OK, so now, and this gets very
weird, but it's like this.

00:46:43.700 --> 00:46:46.660
You'd say, OK, let's take
this Lagrangian, which

00:46:46.660 --> 00:46:50.380
is sort of an attempt
to basically handle

00:46:50.380 --> 00:46:53.650
the constraints for a
problem in the objective.

00:46:53.650 --> 00:46:55.900
But you're doing it in a
weird price-directed way.

00:46:55.900 --> 00:46:58.358
You're just saying there's a
price for each of these things

00:46:58.358 --> 00:47:00.563
and you can violate
them, blah, blah, blah.

00:47:00.563 --> 00:47:01.480
So it looks like that.

00:47:01.480 --> 00:47:05.380
OK, now, what we're
going to do is

00:47:05.380 --> 00:47:07.760
we're going to define
the dual function.

00:47:07.760 --> 00:47:11.050
And it's going to be a function
of these Lagrange multipliers.

00:47:11.050 --> 00:47:13.392
They're also called
dual variables.

00:47:13.392 --> 00:47:15.100
By the way, they're
also called all sorts

00:47:15.100 --> 00:47:17.530
in many application areas.

00:47:17.530 --> 00:47:22.060
They have specific names
in that application area.

00:47:22.060 --> 00:47:24.700
In many, they're
called shadow prices.

00:47:24.700 --> 00:47:26.350
Or, for example,
in energy, they're

00:47:26.350 --> 00:47:28.300
called locational marginal.

00:47:28.300 --> 00:47:32.740
Locational marginal prices,
is what they're called.

00:47:32.740 --> 00:47:34.930
In a lot of fields, these
things just have names.

00:47:37.510 --> 00:47:44.860
So this thing is the least cost
here, over all your choices

00:47:44.860 --> 00:47:48.490
with these Lagrange
multipliers, which we'll later

00:47:48.490 --> 00:47:51.430
see interpreted as prices.

00:47:51.430 --> 00:47:54.280
You minimize that and
whatever the minimum is,

00:47:54.280 --> 00:47:55.930
that's called this
dual function.

00:47:55.930 --> 00:47:57.430
You saw something like that.

00:47:57.430 --> 00:47:58.930
It was a conjugate
function, if you

00:47:58.930 --> 00:48:01.570
remember that one interpretation
of a conjugate function

00:48:01.570 --> 00:48:05.650
was one is you interpreted
y as a vector of prices.

00:48:05.650 --> 00:48:08.800
And then the
conjugate function was

00:48:08.800 --> 00:48:12.640
the optimal cost,
the minimal cost,

00:48:12.640 --> 00:48:14.108
as a function of the prices.

00:48:14.108 --> 00:48:16.150
I don't know if you remember
that interpretation.

00:48:16.150 --> 00:48:17.660
This is exactly that.

00:48:17.660 --> 00:48:20.500
If you think of these as
prices, which you will shortly,

00:48:20.500 --> 00:48:24.400
then basically the dual
function is the optimal cost

00:48:24.400 --> 00:48:28.450
as a function of the prices.

00:48:28.450 --> 00:48:34.330
OK, now we're going to make some
extremely simple observations.

00:48:34.330 --> 00:48:35.395
The first is this.

00:48:38.040 --> 00:48:41.370
The first one is this
function. g is concave.

00:48:41.370 --> 00:48:43.110
The dual function is concave.

00:48:43.110 --> 00:48:47.730
Even when the original
problem is not convex,

00:48:47.730 --> 00:48:49.410
it's just concave.

00:48:49.410 --> 00:48:52.110
Actually, somebody
want to explain why?

00:48:52.110 --> 00:48:53.745
Why Is g concave?

00:48:58.660 --> 00:49:01.390
A minimum of convex functions.

00:49:01.390 --> 00:49:04.390
Yeah, it's a minimal
of affine functions,

00:49:04.390 --> 00:49:05.590
which promote to convex.

00:49:05.590 --> 00:49:08.770
Yeah, if you look
at the Lagrangian,

00:49:08.770 --> 00:49:12.430
it is an affine function
of lambda and nu.

00:49:12.430 --> 00:49:14.290
If you consider lambda
and nu the variables,

00:49:14.290 --> 00:49:15.370
it's an affine function.

00:49:15.370 --> 00:49:16.990
Now it might be
really complicated,

00:49:16.990 --> 00:49:18.740
because I don't know
that's for any that's

00:49:18.740 --> 00:49:20.350
for any particular value of x.

00:49:20.350 --> 00:49:21.640
It's an affine function.

00:49:21.640 --> 00:49:24.250
This says, just take
the infimum or minimum

00:49:24.250 --> 00:49:26.830
over a family of affine
functions that's concave,

00:49:26.830 --> 00:49:27.700
period.

00:49:27.700 --> 00:49:29.530
OK, so the first
one is this thing

00:49:29.530 --> 00:49:31.720
is concave, no matter what.

00:49:31.720 --> 00:49:32.290
Always.

00:49:32.290 --> 00:49:33.070
Just period.

00:49:33.070 --> 00:49:35.080
OK, second one is weird.

00:49:35.080 --> 00:49:39.010
And it looks very
deep and you will soon

00:49:39.010 --> 00:49:43.240
