happens is if this is non-zero,
the minimum is minus infinity.

01:01:54.500 --> 01:01:58.863
Otherwise, I assume this is zero
and it's equal to that thing.

01:01:58.863 --> 01:01:59.780
So this is what I get.

01:02:03.060 --> 01:02:08.640
So g is, in fact,
a linear function

01:02:08.640 --> 01:02:11.910
restricted to a hyperplane.

01:02:11.910 --> 01:02:16.260
That's not a hyperplane,
an affine set that

01:02:16.260 --> 01:02:18.960
is in fact a concave function.

01:02:18.960 --> 01:02:20.760
It kind of has to be, right.

01:02:20.760 --> 01:02:21.930
And you know what?

01:02:21.930 --> 01:02:23.550
This says I'm going
to rewrite this

01:02:23.550 --> 01:02:26.910
as that A-transpose nu
minus lambda plus c is zero.

01:02:26.910 --> 01:02:29.190
That means A-transpose
nu plus c equals lambda.

01:02:29.190 --> 01:02:31.552
Lambda is anything that
is any parameter that's

01:02:31.552 --> 01:02:32.760
bigger than or equal to zero.

01:02:32.760 --> 01:02:34.950
And then, I get this
lower bound property.

01:02:34.950 --> 01:02:35.670
It says this.

01:02:35.670 --> 01:02:38.190
It says, you look at
this linear program

01:02:38.190 --> 01:02:40.800
and it says, yeah,
you know what.

01:02:40.800 --> 01:02:43.950
The optimal value of
this thing is bigger than

01:02:43.950 --> 01:02:47.190
or equal to minus b-transpose
nu if for any nu that

01:02:47.190 --> 01:02:49.837
satisfies A-transpose
nu plus c is

01:02:49.837 --> 01:02:51.045
bigger than or equal to zero.

01:02:54.700 --> 01:02:57.670
And that just came straight
from the dual function.

01:02:57.670 --> 01:02:59.800
Now, it turns out
for a lot of these,

01:02:59.800 --> 01:03:01.880
you can just show it
directly and it's not hard.

01:03:01.880 --> 01:03:03.920
I mean, I could do let
me do it for that one.

01:03:03.920 --> 01:03:05.795
And then I don't think
I'll ever do it again,

01:03:05.795 --> 01:03:07.130
but I'll do it for that one.

01:03:07.130 --> 01:03:08.150
Let's do that.

01:03:08.150 --> 01:03:15.730
So my original problem is
minimize c-transpose x.

01:03:15.730 --> 01:03:18.220
And then subject
to, I think it's

01:03:18.220 --> 01:03:23.140
Ax equals b and x is
bigger than zero, right.

01:03:23.140 --> 01:03:24.880
And then the claim
is if somebody

01:03:24.880 --> 01:03:30.490
walks up to me on the street and
says, I have a vector nu and--

01:03:30.490 --> 01:03:36.340
what is it-- A-transpose nu
plus c is bigger than zero,

01:03:36.340 --> 01:03:43.973
they claim then this implies
that minus b-transpose--

01:03:43.973 --> 01:03:45.640
sorry you have to
look at it over here--

01:03:45.640 --> 01:03:49.300
nu is a lower bound on p-star.

01:03:52.570 --> 01:03:54.412
So this is pretty cool, right.

01:03:54.412 --> 01:03:55.370
So that's what it says.

01:03:58.335 --> 01:04:00.710
If we just asked, so you came
up to someone on the street

01:04:00.710 --> 01:04:02.210
or now if we put
that on a homework,

01:04:02.210 --> 01:04:03.740
you couldn't you
couldn't do this.

01:04:03.740 --> 01:04:06.380
How would you show that?

01:04:06.380 --> 01:04:08.090
It looks very deep,
but in fact, it

01:04:08.090 --> 01:04:11.420
used absolutely nothing but
the completely trivial steps

01:04:11.420 --> 01:04:12.530
I had before.

01:04:12.530 --> 01:04:16.460
So let's just show this
directly, just for fun.

01:04:16.460 --> 01:04:17.870
So here's what
we're going to do.

01:04:17.870 --> 01:04:20.990
Suppose x is feasible.

01:04:20.990 --> 01:04:24.005
Any x is feasible that
means Ax equals bx,

01:04:24.005 --> 01:04:28.010
x is bigger than
or equal to zero.

01:04:28.010 --> 01:04:34.850
If that's the case, then let
me look at this, nu-transpose.

01:04:34.850 --> 01:04:39.420
Then this must be true,
right, because x is feasible.

01:04:39.420 --> 01:04:42.642
So Ax equals b, so this
surely holds, right.

01:04:42.642 --> 01:04:44.600
Oh, and I'm assuming
somebody came up with a nu

01:04:44.600 --> 01:04:45.433
that satisfies this.

01:04:45.433 --> 01:04:46.550
Everybody agree with that?

01:04:46.550 --> 01:04:47.990
I mean you kind
of have to, right?

01:04:47.990 --> 01:04:50.180
It's like simple stuff.

01:04:50.180 --> 01:04:54.200
OK, but then I'm going
to write this thing.

01:04:54.200 --> 01:04:57.860
This says that A-transpose here.

01:04:57.860 --> 01:05:04.460
This is A-transpose nu plus c
is bigger than or equal to zero.

01:05:04.460 --> 01:05:06.200
We agree with that, right.

01:05:06.200 --> 01:05:09.260
Now x is also bigger
than zero, and I'm

01:05:09.260 --> 01:05:10.940
going to use an
extremely deep fact

01:05:10.940 --> 01:05:14.180
The inner product of
two non-negative vectors

01:05:14.180 --> 01:05:15.170
is non-negative.

01:05:18.050 --> 01:05:21.110
It's just not hard.

01:05:21.110 --> 01:05:25.115
I'll put this on the other side,
so I'll write nu-transpose A

01:05:25.115 --> 01:05:31.670
and I'll multiply, let's
see, plus c-transpose x.

01:05:31.670 --> 01:05:33.650
That's bigger than
or equal to zero.

01:05:33.650 --> 01:05:34.985
Everyone agrees with that?

01:05:34.985 --> 01:05:37.610
And I'm just using the fact that
that's an inner product of two

01:05:37.610 --> 01:05:38.850
non-negative vectors.

01:05:38.850 --> 01:05:40.400
So you have this.

01:05:40.400 --> 01:05:45.260
OK, expand this out,
you get nu-transpose Ax

01:05:45.260 --> 01:05:48.185
plus c-transpose x
is bigger than zero.

01:05:51.160 --> 01:05:52.270
But wait a minute.

01:05:52.270 --> 01:05:56.230
This thing is equal
to nu-transpose b.

01:05:56.230 --> 01:05:59.920
So this is nu-transpose b.

01:05:59.920 --> 01:06:05.140
And then I put this over here
and I have minus nu-transpose.

01:06:05.140 --> 01:06:08.360
And I get this.

01:06:08.360 --> 01:06:10.240
So here's what I
just showed you.

01:06:10.240 --> 01:06:12.430
I said take any
x that's feasible

01:06:12.430 --> 01:06:14.630
for this linear program.

01:06:14.630 --> 01:06:18.118
And what I showed you is I don't
know what c-transpose x is.

01:06:18.118 --> 01:06:19.660
But I'll tell you
one thing about it.

01:06:19.660 --> 01:06:21.970
It cannot be smaller than
minus nu-transpose b.

01:06:21.970 --> 01:06:24.640
So everyone see that
I just completely

01:06:24.640 --> 01:06:28.550
derived it from scratch with
none of this Lagrangian, blah,

01:06:28.550 --> 01:06:29.050
blah, blah.

01:06:29.050 --> 01:06:29.890
And dual function.

01:06:29.890 --> 01:06:32.660
Everybody see it?

01:06:32.660 --> 01:06:34.760
So that's how that goes.

01:06:34.760 --> 01:06:37.970
Now this is already
kind of interesting.

01:06:37.970 --> 01:06:39.740
As a matter of
fact, later, here's

01:06:39.740 --> 01:06:40.823
what you're going to find.

01:06:40.823 --> 01:06:43.280
It's pretty awesome.

01:06:43.280 --> 01:06:46.760
When we do a numerical
solution of an LP,

01:06:46.760 --> 01:06:48.050
here's what's going to happen.

01:06:48.050 --> 01:06:52.620
When you solve that LP,
you will get x back,

01:06:52.620 --> 01:06:57.750
whether you like it or not,
you're also going to get a nu.

01:06:57.750 --> 01:07:00.690
And this is going to be
true for most all the convex

01:07:00.690 --> 01:07:04.403
problems you solve,
which is amazing,

01:07:04.403 --> 01:07:06.320
because you're going to
get a nu and the bound

01:07:06.320 --> 01:07:08.067
is going to be tight.

01:07:08.067 --> 01:07:09.150
So what's going to happen.

01:07:09.150 --> 01:07:11.250
So your model of a solver
is going to look like this.

01:07:11.250 --> 01:07:12.000
Here's my problem.

01:07:12.000 --> 01:07:13.100
And they go, no problem.

01:07:13.100 --> 01:07:14.990
They say, here's x.

01:07:14.990 --> 01:07:18.830
You check it's feasible and its
objective value is like 3.7.

01:07:18.830 --> 01:07:21.380
Everybody following this?

01:07:21.380 --> 01:07:26.030
You will also be given what's
called a dual certificate.

01:07:26.030 --> 01:07:29.630
A dual certificate is, it'll
give you dual variables, which

01:07:29.630 --> 01:07:33.020
if you evaluate the dual
objective, which is g,

01:07:33.020 --> 01:07:36.230
you would get a lower
bound on the optimal value.

01:07:36.230 --> 01:07:37.340
Everybody following this?

01:07:37.340 --> 01:07:39.140
When you evaluate
it, it's actually

01:07:39.140 --> 01:07:42.860
equal to the prime
value you were given,

01:07:42.860 --> 01:07:43.988
the objective value.

01:07:43.988 --> 01:07:44.780
Everybody see this?

01:07:44.780 --> 01:07:46.820
That's called a
dual certificate.

01:07:46.820 --> 01:07:51.650
So it means you don't even
have to trust the solver.

01:07:51.650 --> 01:07:54.020
It basically,
abstractly, I think

01:07:54.020 --> 01:07:55.640
it's beautiful it
actually returns

01:07:55.640 --> 01:08:00.710
two things, an optimal point
and a very, short self-contained

01:08:00.710 --> 01:08:04.340
proof that no one
could do better.

01:08:04.340 --> 01:08:05.210
Everybody got it?

01:08:05.210 --> 01:08:07.190
That's what a dual
certificate is.

01:08:07.190 --> 01:08:09.170
We'll talk much more
about this later.

01:08:09.170 --> 01:08:12.320
But it's super
interesting, right.

01:08:12.320 --> 01:08:14.135
It's making sense, vaguely?

01:08:18.500 --> 01:08:19.189
Here's one.

01:08:19.189 --> 01:08:22.220
We can do a quality-constrained
minimization.

01:08:22.220 --> 01:08:24.560
So here I just have a
general norm, right.

01:08:24.560 --> 01:08:27.170
So I'm going to minimize a
norm subject to Ax equals b.

01:08:27.170 --> 01:08:30.680
This is the Lagrangian.

01:08:30.680 --> 01:08:33.859
I take the norm
minus and then I have

01:08:33.859 --> 01:08:37.229
nu-transpose times Ax
minus b, or, in this case,

01:08:37.229 --> 01:08:40.310
I wrote it as b minus
Ax, and I get this thing.

01:08:40.310 --> 01:08:43.130
And then we have
to minimize this.

01:08:43.130 --> 01:08:45.270
Now, by the way, if
you look at this,

01:08:45.270 --> 01:08:47.899
you'll already see
something clear.

01:08:47.899 --> 01:08:49.910
b-transpose nu has
nothing to do with x.

01:08:49.910 --> 01:08:52.430
So that comes out
of the infimum here,

01:08:52.430 --> 01:08:56.000
but here this is a norm
minus a linear function.

01:08:56.000 --> 01:09:00.660
That's actually the
conjugate function.

01:09:00.660 --> 01:09:01.771
So what happens?

01:09:01.771 --> 01:09:03.229
And you can also
work out what that

01:09:03.229 --> 01:09:07.569
is, the norm minus
a linear function.

01:09:07.569 --> 01:09:09.390
When I minimize
that, I either get 0

01:09:09.390 --> 01:09:12.630
or I get minus infinity,
right, and so here you

01:09:12.630 --> 01:09:13.532
would get this thing.

01:09:13.532 --> 01:09:15.240
And again I'm not
going over the details.

01:09:15.240 --> 01:09:16.240
I would let you do that.

01:09:16.240 --> 01:09:18.960
But that you'd get
something like that.

01:09:18.960 --> 01:09:22.380
So the dual function is this
linear function b-transpose nu,

01:09:22.380 --> 01:09:27.630
provided A-transpose nu in the
dual norm is less than one.

01:09:27.630 --> 01:09:30.950
So that's what you get.

01:09:30.950 --> 01:09:33.649
Then, it says this.

01:09:33.649 --> 01:09:35.630
You want to solve this problem.

01:09:35.630 --> 01:09:37.609
Then here's a lower bound.

01:09:37.609 --> 01:09:40.850
It says a lower bound is
b-transpose nu for any nu that

01:09:40.850 --> 01:09:43.450
satisfies this, right.

01:09:43.450 --> 01:09:48.279
In the dual norm right so let's
suppose this is a minimum fuel

01:09:48.279 --> 01:09:50.361
trajectory generation
problem and you

01:09:50.361 --> 01:09:51.819
want to take a
satellite from where

01:09:51.819 --> 01:09:53.680
it is to where it
is supposed to be

01:09:53.680 --> 01:09:55.210
and you want to
use minimum fuel,

01:09:55.210 --> 01:09:57.790
this would probably be
something like an l1 norm.

01:09:57.790 --> 01:09:59.860
OK, that's your job.

01:09:59.860 --> 01:10:02.530
You work this out.

01:10:02.530 --> 01:10:09.520
And what's cool is this says,
if I take any vector at all

01:10:09.520 --> 01:10:15.670
and it satisfies this, then if I
evaluate b-transpose nu, that's

01:10:15.670 --> 01:10:18.370
a lower bound on the
minimum amount of fuel

01:10:18.370 --> 01:10:20.710
it's going to take you
to get from where you are

01:10:20.710 --> 01:10:22.450
to where you're supposed to be.

01:10:22.450 --> 01:10:23.527
Everybody got this?

01:10:23.527 --> 01:10:25.360
So it's already getting
kind of interesting.

01:10:25.360 --> 01:10:27.580
And there should be
at least very strong

01:10:27.580 --> 01:10:30.290
hints that a lot of this
is unbelievably useful.

01:10:30.290 --> 01:10:32.810
So everybody got this?

01:10:32.810 --> 01:10:34.232
So this is not obvious, right.

01:10:34.232 --> 01:10:35.440
These things are not obvious.

01:10:35.440 --> 01:10:37.870
I could give just a
simple proof directly

01:10:37.870 --> 01:10:39.350
that this is a lower bound.

01:10:39.350 --> 01:10:41.250
And so on.

01:10:41.250 --> 01:10:44.060
Now it's about to get even
more interesting, because we're

01:10:44.060 --> 01:10:45.360
going to look at a problem.

01:10:45.360 --> 01:10:47.430
Well, it'll come up again.

01:10:47.430 --> 01:10:50.450
So it's two-way partitioning.

01:10:50.450 --> 01:10:53.825
So this is the two way
partitioning problem.

01:10:56.540 --> 01:11:03.950
I have a set of n objects
and I want to partition them

01:11:03.950 --> 01:11:07.070
into to two groups.

01:11:07.070 --> 01:11:11.090
And the way I'll do that is I'll
encode it with a vector x that

01:11:11.090 --> 01:11:14.690
has plus one if
xi equals plus 1,

01:11:14.690 --> 01:11:17.780
which means item i is
in the first group.

01:11:17.780 --> 01:11:20.890
xi equals minus one if
you're in the second group.

01:11:20.890 --> 01:11:21.640
Everybody got it?

01:11:21.640 --> 01:11:27.340
So that says that each x is
either plus or minus one.

01:11:27.340 --> 01:11:29.200
And then my objective
is this thing.

01:11:29.200 --> 01:11:31.030
And to get a rough
idea, we should actually

01:11:31.030 --> 01:11:34.240
say what x transpose
Wx is it's because it's

01:11:34.240 --> 01:11:36.640
an interesting problem
and maybe you've

01:11:36.640 --> 01:11:38.960
seen it in some other class
or something like that.

01:11:38.960 --> 01:11:42.820
But it's worth
saying what it is.

01:11:42.820 --> 01:11:44.230
It's x transpose Wx?

01:11:44.230 --> 01:11:44.800
Yeah.

01:11:44.800 --> 01:11:50.350
OK so it's x-transpose Wx,
but that's equal to this.

01:11:50.350 --> 01:11:57.910
It's the sum over i
and j of W i j xi xj.

01:11:57.910 --> 01:11:59.300
I mean so we all
agree with that.

01:11:59.300 --> 01:12:00.790
That's a quadratic form.

01:12:00.790 --> 01:12:04.300
Now xi and xj are each
plus or minus one.

01:12:04.300 --> 01:12:07.420
So this product is plus one.

01:12:07.420 --> 01:12:10.720
If items i and j are
in the same group

01:12:10.720 --> 01:12:14.570
and it's minus one if
they're in different groups.

01:12:14.570 --> 01:12:19.270
And so this is equal to
the sum of the W's when

01:12:19.270 --> 01:12:22.600
they're in the same group
minus the sum of W's when

01:12:22.600 --> 01:12:24.323
they're in different groups.

01:12:24.323 --> 01:12:25.240
That's what this says.

01:12:25.240 --> 01:12:27.350
And so you should interpret--
what are we doing?

01:12:27.350 --> 01:12:28.683
Are we maximizing or minimizing?

01:12:28.683 --> 01:12:29.800
We are minimizing.

01:12:29.800 --> 01:12:32.470
So if we're minimizing
this, it basically

01:12:32.470 --> 01:12:36.340
says W i is the cost
of having, let's

01:12:36.340 --> 01:12:39.580
say you're doing we're going
to split a group of people

01:12:39.580 --> 01:12:43.750
into two groups right
so this says if Wij

01:12:43.750 --> 01:12:48.010
is the cost of having person i
and person j in the same team.

01:12:48.010 --> 01:12:49.360
If we're making two teams.

01:12:49.360 --> 01:12:50.750
Everybody got this?

01:12:50.750 --> 01:12:54.280
So if Wij is positive, it means
they don't like each other

01:12:54.280 --> 01:12:55.730
if it's negative.

01:12:55.730 --> 01:12:56.620
That's totally cool.

01:12:56.620 --> 01:12:58.060
It means there's
a strong affinity

01:12:58.060 --> 01:13:00.018
and we would like them
to be in the same group.

01:13:00.018 --> 01:13:01.150
Everybody got it?

01:13:01.150 --> 01:13:03.580
And the same is true for--

01:13:03.580 --> 01:13:06.280
basically, you have an
equal and opposite charge.

01:13:06.280 --> 01:13:10.240
So if you say Wij
is plus three, it

01:13:10.240 --> 01:13:14.620
means you will pay
three if you assign i

01:13:14.620 --> 01:13:16.360
and j to the same group.

01:13:16.360 --> 01:13:20.230
And you will have a
revenue or income of three,

01:13:20.230 --> 01:13:23.155
if it'll become minus three.

01:13:23.155 --> 01:13:25.280
And if it's minus three,
it's the other way around,

01:13:25.280 --> 01:13:29.630
so the W's specify how
we like how much it

01:13:29.630 --> 01:13:32.097
irritates us to have
items in the same group

01:13:32.097 --> 01:13:32.930
or different groups.

01:13:35.960 --> 01:13:39.120
So this is the problem.

01:13:39.120 --> 01:13:41.250
OK, so let's look at
the dual function.

01:13:41.250 --> 01:13:45.000
It's obviously non-convex,
for two reasons.

01:13:45.000 --> 01:13:47.520
W need not be positive
semi-definite.

01:13:47.520 --> 01:13:50.490
We didn't say that it was.

01:13:50.490 --> 01:13:53.220
Reason two is we
got a big old fail

01:13:53.220 --> 01:13:58.770
right there right because we
have squares equal to one.

01:13:58.770 --> 01:14:02.790
By the way, if I take xi squared
less than or equal to one,

01:14:02.790 --> 01:14:03.900
that's convex.

01:14:03.900 --> 01:14:08.700
If W is positive, semi-definite.

01:14:08.700 --> 01:14:10.620
We take the dual function.

01:14:10.620 --> 01:14:13.350
We take the objective plus.

01:14:13.350 --> 01:14:15.360
And then I just
simply add up nu-i

01:14:15.360 --> 01:14:17.220
times these xi
squared minus one.

01:14:17.220 --> 01:14:21.070
And what I end up with
is a quadratic function.

01:14:21.070 --> 01:14:25.080
A quadratic form in
x plus a constant.

01:14:25.080 --> 01:14:28.020
OK, and now we're going
to have our quadratic form

01:14:28.020 --> 01:14:29.235
minimization.

01:14:29.235 --> 01:14:31.860
Earlier today we had our how do
you minimize an affine function

01:14:31.860 --> 01:14:32.640
conversation.

01:14:32.640 --> 01:14:35.930
Now we're going to have
quadratic form minimization

01:14:35.930 --> 01:14:36.800
conversation.

01:14:36.800 --> 01:14:40.085
So if you minimize a quadratic
form, what do you get?

01:14:43.533 --> 01:14:45.200
Someone walks up to
the street and says,

01:14:45.200 --> 01:14:50.900
here's my x-transpose,
not W, But x-transpose.

01:14:50.900 --> 01:14:54.920
Ax, A is symmetric what's
the minimum of that?

01:14:59.570 --> 01:15:01.577
Well, if that matrix
has a negative,

01:15:01.577 --> 01:15:03.410
it's got real eigenvalues,
symmetric, right?

01:15:03.410 --> 01:15:05.300
If that matrix has a
negative eigenvalue,

01:15:05.300 --> 01:15:07.430
that minimum is, for
sure, minus infinity,

01:15:07.430 --> 01:15:12.555
because you sail out along that,
find a negative eigenvalue,

01:15:12.555 --> 01:15:14.180
for that matter just
find a point where

01:15:14.180 --> 01:15:16.310
the quadratic form is negative.

01:15:16.310 --> 01:15:18.935
If you just go out
farther and farther,

01:15:18.935 --> 01:15:20.310
it'll get more
and more negative.

01:15:20.310 --> 01:15:24.200
In fact, quadratically so, then
the minimum is minus infinity.

01:15:24.200 --> 01:15:25.430
Everybody got it?

01:15:25.430 --> 01:15:27.980
But if that matrix is
positive, semi-definite.

01:15:27.980 --> 01:15:29.570
And I ask, what's the minimum?

01:15:29.570 --> 01:15:30.810
The answer is real simple.

01:15:30.810 --> 01:15:33.910
It's zero, because
you could take zero

01:15:33.910 --> 01:15:37.480
as your x equals
zero, but because it's

01:15:37.480 --> 01:15:39.370
positive semi-definite,
you can't get it.

01:15:39.370 --> 01:15:40.880
It can't be anything
less than that.

01:15:40.880 --> 01:15:41.710
So that's it.

01:15:41.710 --> 01:15:42.980
Everybody follow that.

01:15:42.980 --> 01:15:44.830
So g is this function.

01:15:44.830 --> 01:15:48.970
It's minus one transpose nu
provided W plus diag of nu

01:15:48.970 --> 01:15:50.320
is bigger than or equal to zero.

01:15:50.320 --> 01:15:55.180
That's in a symmetric, in
a symmetric matrix sense.

01:15:55.180 --> 01:16:00.670
OK, and the lower bound property
says you see this problem here,

01:16:00.670 --> 01:16:02.240
you know what.

01:16:02.240 --> 01:16:05.680
Here's a bound on its objective.

01:16:05.680 --> 01:16:06.992
It is.

01:16:06.992 --> 01:16:08.950
That's a lower bound on
its minus one transpose

01:16:08.950 --> 01:16:12.790
nu, provided for any nu you
can find that satisfies this.

01:16:12.790 --> 01:16:13.360
OK?

01:16:13.360 --> 01:16:17.560
So that's what that says.

01:16:17.560 --> 01:16:20.260
And it turns out you can get
all sorts of famous bounds out

01:16:20.260 --> 01:16:25.120
of this by just plugging
numbers in, but that's the idea.

01:16:25.120 --> 01:16:29.350
And this is related to
spectral partitioning.

01:16:29.350 --> 01:16:32.900
How many people have seen that?

01:16:32.900 --> 01:16:33.470
One hand.

01:16:33.470 --> 01:16:34.160
Just a hand.

01:16:34.160 --> 01:16:36.890
Oh, I'm sorry,
everybody needs to know

01:16:36.890 --> 01:16:38.690
about spectral partitioning.

01:16:38.690 --> 01:16:41.390
You're not going to be able to
do any serious street fighting

01:16:41.390 --> 01:16:44.090
applied math if you
don't know about that,

01:16:44.090 --> 01:16:47.090
because partitioning
problems come up all the time

01:16:47.090 --> 01:16:49.170
and you need to know about it.

01:16:49.170 --> 01:16:52.250
I'll just show you quickly,
what it is here, just for fun.

01:16:52.250 --> 01:16:56.900
I won't go into the details, but
it's actually weirdly another--

01:16:56.900 --> 01:16:58.675
Let me just explain
it very quickly.

01:17:01.520 --> 01:17:02.450
I won't go into that--

01:17:02.450 --> 01:17:07.820
I'll give a very
simple version of it.

01:17:07.820 --> 01:17:13.250
So you have this
thing and then we're

01:17:13.250 --> 01:17:18.530
going to minimize this
thing, subject to xi squared

01:17:18.530 --> 01:17:19.490
equals one.

01:17:19.490 --> 01:17:22.520
That's not convex, right?

01:17:22.520 --> 01:17:26.240
But if this is true,
if xi squared is one,

01:17:26.240 --> 01:17:28.850
then everyone here would
have to, I would hope,

01:17:28.850 --> 01:17:31.910
agree that that's the case.

01:17:31.910 --> 01:17:34.380
Now, by the way,
this is a relaxation.

01:17:34.380 --> 01:17:38.310
Because if the former constraint
holds, then this one has to.

01:17:38.310 --> 01:17:39.630
OK W is symmetric.

01:17:39.630 --> 01:17:43.200
Anybody take a look at that
how to solve that problem.

01:17:43.200 --> 01:17:44.325
First of all, is it convex?

01:17:47.290 --> 01:17:47.860
That convex.

01:17:50.480 --> 01:17:52.130
No.

01:17:52.130 --> 01:17:54.080
Because of this, right.

01:17:54.080 --> 01:17:56.270
If that was less than or
equal to, it'd be convex.

01:17:56.270 --> 01:17:58.520
But this is not convex.

01:17:58.520 --> 01:18:02.540
This is one of the-- there's
about seven problems we

01:18:02.540 --> 01:18:04.220
can solve that are not convex.

01:18:04.220 --> 01:18:05.430
This is one of them.

01:18:05.430 --> 01:18:07.430
It's called it's just
an eigenvalue problem.

01:18:07.430 --> 01:18:18.210
What's the solution of this,
in terms of the eigenvectors?

01:18:18.210 --> 01:18:19.150
What's that?

01:18:19.150 --> 01:18:20.130
The smallest one.

01:18:20.130 --> 01:18:21.690
Yeah, we know how to do this.

01:18:21.690 --> 01:18:24.690
You find the smallest
eigenvalue of W.

01:18:24.690 --> 01:18:26.250
You find its
associated eigenvector

01:18:26.250 --> 01:18:27.810
and you like scale it by n.

01:18:27.810 --> 01:18:30.480
And that's literally
the solution.

01:18:30.480 --> 01:18:34.470
That's not a heuristic
or anything like that.

01:18:34.470 --> 01:18:36.990
Oh, by the way,
when you do that--

01:18:36.990 --> 01:18:39.060
what have I done here my God--

01:18:39.060 --> 01:18:45.450
OK, when you do that, you get
something really interesting.

01:18:45.450 --> 01:18:48.420
First of all, you get a number
because this is a relaxation.

01:18:48.420 --> 01:18:51.150
That's a lower bound on
the original problem.

01:18:51.150 --> 01:18:52.770
But then you also get this.

01:18:52.770 --> 01:18:57.400
I get an x's which are all
they're not they satisfy this.

01:18:57.400 --> 01:18:59.490
But they're definitely
not all plus, minus one.

01:18:59.490 --> 01:19:01.380
Somebody want to
make a suggestion?

01:19:01.380 --> 01:19:05.490
To make a heuristic
assignment at that point?

01:19:05.490 --> 01:19:06.240
You have a vector.

01:19:06.240 --> 01:19:07.710
They're all floats.

01:19:07.710 --> 01:19:10.600
Their sum of their squares is n.

01:19:10.600 --> 01:19:12.600
But the original problem
you wanted all the xi's

01:19:12.600 --> 01:19:13.600
to be plus or minus one.

01:19:13.600 --> 01:19:16.400
Someone please
make a suggestion.

01:19:16.400 --> 01:19:17.120
Round them.

01:19:17.120 --> 01:19:17.960
Round them, done.

01:19:17.960 --> 01:19:19.550
Take the sign.

01:19:19.550 --> 01:19:20.510
Done.

01:19:20.510 --> 01:19:21.660
Congratulations.

01:19:21.660 --> 01:19:24.675
That is spectral partitioning.

01:19:24.675 --> 01:19:26.550
Except for one thing I
didn't tell you about,

01:19:26.550 --> 01:19:29.420
which is we can adjust
the diagonal of W

01:19:29.420 --> 01:19:31.310
in a way that will make
that slightly better.

01:19:31.310 --> 01:19:33.500
That's spectral partitioning.

01:19:33.500 --> 01:19:37.400
This works for
gigantic problems.

01:19:37.400 --> 01:19:39.890
It works super well,
so there you go.

01:19:39.890 --> 01:19:40.610
Yeah.

01:19:40.610 --> 01:19:43.820
So in this problem, W has
to be symmetric or positive

01:19:43.820 --> 01:19:44.430
semi-definite.

01:19:44.430 --> 01:19:45.680
No, no, no, no, no, no, no.

01:19:45.680 --> 01:19:47.630
In this problem, well, OK.

01:19:47.630 --> 01:19:49.370
Non-symmetric doesn't
even make sense,

01:19:49.370 --> 01:19:51.290
although just because you're
just replacing W with its

01:19:51.290 --> 01:19:52.100
quadratic part--

01:19:52.100 --> 01:19:53.750
I mean sorry, its
symmetric part--

01:19:53.750 --> 01:19:55.970
if I have a quadratic
form and I replace

01:19:55.970 --> 01:19:58.310
the matrix non-symmetric,
I just replace

01:19:58.310 --> 01:20:00.800
that matrix with that
matrix plus its transpose

01:20:00.800 --> 01:20:01.702
divided by two.

01:20:01.702 --> 01:20:04.160
Number one, then does it have
to be positive semi-definite?

01:20:04.160 --> 01:20:06.140
Absolutely not.

01:20:06.140 --> 01:20:11.210
We know this problem
we can solve completely

01:20:11.210 --> 01:20:12.475
as long as W is symmetric.

01:20:12.475 --> 01:20:13.850
It can have negative
eigenvalues,

01:20:13.850 --> 01:20:15.558
positive eigenvalues,
couldn't care less.

01:20:15.558 --> 01:20:19.740
The solution to this is
x is a scaled version

01:20:19.740 --> 01:20:23.120
of the minimizing
eigenvector, right.

01:20:23.120 --> 01:20:25.640
Is that OK?

